add_q("inhouse_196", CAT7,
"A neurophysiologist is comparing the four major classes of cutaneous mechanoreceptors in human glabrous (hairless) skin. Which mechanoreceptor is located superficially in the dermal papillae, has a small receptive field with distinct borders, adapts rapidly to sustained stimuli, and is specialized for detecting low-frequency vibration (flutter, 30-50 Hz) and minute skin slip for grip control?",
["Meissner corpuscle", "Merkel cell-neurite complex", "Pacinian corpuscle", "Ruffini ending", "Free nerve ending"],
0,
"The four principal cutaneous mechanoreceptors have distinct anatomical locations and physiological properties:\n1) MEISSNER CORPUSCLES: Located superficially in the DERMAL PAPILLAE of glabrous skin (fingertips, palms, lips). They have SMALL RECEPTIVE FIELDS with sharp borders and are RAPIDLY ADAPTING (fire only at stimulus onset and offset). They respond optimally to LOW-FREQUENCY VIBRATION (FLUTTER, 30-50 Hz) and detect micro-slips between skin and objects, mediating dynamic grip adjustment;\n2) MERKEL DISCS: Superficial, SLOWLY ADAPTING TYPE 1 (SA1), small receptive fields, detect sustained pressure, edges, and fine texture (Braille);\n3) PACINIAN CORPUSCLES: Deep dermis/subcutis, RAPIDLY ADAPTING, large diffuse receptive fields, high-frequency vibration (200-300 Hz);\n4) RUFFINI ENDINGS: Deep dermis, SLOWLY ADAPTING TYPE 2 (SA2), large fields, detect skin stretch.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Merkel discs are slowly adapting (SA1).\n- Pacinian corpuscles are deep with large receptive fields and detect high-frequency vibration.\n- Ruffini endings are slowly adapting and detect skin stretch.\n- Free nerve endings detect pain, temperature, and crude touch.")

add_q("inhouse_197", CAT7,
"While holding a delicate crystal wine glass, a man begins to relax his fingers, causing the glass to start slipping downward by a fraction of a millimeter. Within 80 milliseconds, his fingers automatically tighten their grip to prevent the glass from dropping. Which sensory receptor was stimulated by this microscopic shear slip to trigger this rapid reflex grip adjustment?",
["Meissner corpuscles", "Pacinian corpuscles", "Merkel discs", "Ruffini endings", "Golgi tendon organs"],
0,
"Meissner corpuscles are the primary transducers of dynamic skin slip and low-frequency shear forces. Concentrated in enormous numbers in the dermal papillae of human fingertips, they are rapidly adapting mechanoreceptors innervated by fast-conducting A-beta myelinated fibers. When a grasped object begins to slip, the localized micro-vibrations and shear displacement across the papillary ridges activate Meissner corpuscles, generating rapid sensory feedback to spinal and cortical sensorimotor circuits that automatically increases grip force within 70 to 100 milliseconds.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Pacinian corpuscles detect high-frequency vibration (tuning fork), not localized papillary slip.\n- Merkel discs detect static form, curvature, and edges.\n- Ruffini endings detect gross skin stretch and joint movements.\n- Golgi tendon organs measure muscle tension at tendons, not cutaneous friction.")

add_q("inhouse_198", CAT7,
"A blind adult reads a novel transcribed in Braille by gliding her index fingertip across patterns of raised tactile dots spaced 2.5 mm apart. She effortlessly distinguishes adjacent dots and recognizes individual letters. Which mechanoreceptor is located in the basal layer of the epidermis, has a small receptive field, adapts slowly to mechanical indentation, and provides the spatial resolution required for Braille reading?",
["Merkel cell-neurite complex", "Meissner corpuscle", "Pacinian corpuscle", "Ruffini ending", "Muscle spindle"],
0,
"The Merkel cell-neurite complex (Merkel disc) is a SLOWLY ADAPTING TYPE 1 (SA1) mechanoreceptor located in the STRATUM BASALE of the epidermis in both glabrous and hairy skin. Merkel discs have exceptionally SMALL RECEPTIVE FIELDS (2-4 mm) with highly localized, sharp borders. They fire continuously throughout sustained mechanical indentation, with firing frequency directly proportional to the depth of indentation, points, and edges. They provide the highest spatial resolution of all mechanoreceptors, making them the essential transducers for tactile form perception, curvature detection, edge discrimination, and BRAILLE READING.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Meissner corpuscles are rapidly adapting and fire only at the onset/offset of motion, failing to sustain firing during static reading.\n- Pacinian corpuscles have large diffuse receptive fields encompassing an entire finger, precluding resolution of 2.5 mm dots.\n- Ruffini endings have large receptive fields and detect stretch.\n- Muscle spindles sense muscle stretch, not epidermal tactile dots.")

add_q("inhouse_199", CAT7,
"A neurologist tests vibratory sensation by striking a 128 Hz tuning fork and placing its vibrating stem firmly against a patient's distal interphalangeal joint. What deep cutaneous mechanoreceptor, composed of a central unmyelinated axon terminal surrounded by concentric, onion-skin lamellae of connective tissue and fluid, is specialized to detect this high-frequency vibration?",
["Pacinian corpuscle", "Meissner corpuscle", "Merkel disc", "Ruffini cylinder", "Krause end bulb"],
0,
"Pacinian corpuscles (Vater-Pacini corpuscles) are large (1-2 mm), encapsulated mechanoreceptors located deep in the dermis, subcutaneous adipose tissue, periosteum, and joint capsules. They consist of a central A-beta nerve ending surrounded by 20 to 60 CONCENTRIC LAMELLAE of flattened Schwann-related cells separated by fluid ('ONION-SKIN' appearance). They are RAPIDLY ADAPTING and have VERY LARGE RECEPTIVE FIELDS with diffuse borders. The layered lamellar capsule functions as a mechanical high-pass filter that absorbs sustained pressure but transmits rapid mechanical oscillations (optimal frequency 200 to 300 Hz), making them the primary transducers of HIGH-FREQUENCY VIBRATION tested with tuning forks.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Meissner corpuscles are superficial and detect low-frequency flutter (30-50 Hz).\n- Merkel discs are slowly adapting and detect static pressure.\n- Ruffini cylinders detect skin stretch.\n- Krause end bulbs were historically linked to cold, now recognized as mucocutaneous end organs.")

