add_q("inhouse_215", CAT7,
"A professional concert violinist who has practiced the violin for 6 hours daily since age 5 undergoes functional cortical mapping. The cortical territory in her right primary somatosensory cortex representing the digits of her left hand (which fingers the violin strings) is nearly double the size of the corresponding hand area in non-musicians. What neurobiological principle does this illustrate?",
["Cortical plasticity / functional reorganization: somatosensory cortical maps undergo dynamic, activity-dependent expansion with intensive training", "Neurogenesis: rapid division and addition of new cortical pyramidal neurons in adulthood", "Cerebral edema: localized swelling of the parietal cortex", "Demyelination of adjacent cortical territories", "Hypertrophy of meningothelial arachnoid cap cells"],
0,
"Cortical somatosensory maps are not fixed or immutable; they exhibit robust CORTICAL PLASTICITY (functional neuroplasticity). Repetitive, highly skilled sensory training (e.g., intensive string instrument playing or Braille reading) drives activity-dependent synaptic plasticity, unmasking previously silent horizontal cortical connections and strengthening synaptic weights. This results in measurable, long-lasting EXPANSION OF CORTICAL TERRITORIES dedicated to the trained body parts (e.g., enlarged representation of the left digits in a violinist's right S1). Conversely, deafferentation (such as limb amputation) causes adjacent cortical maps (e.g., face area) to invade the deafferented territory, providing the neurobiological basis for phantom limb sensations.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Pyramidal neurons in human neocortex do not divide or undergo significant neurogenesis in adulthood.\n- Plasticity is a physiological synaptic adaptation, not pathological cerebral edema.\n- Demyelination impairs conduction, whereas skilled performance requires intact, strengthened myelin.\n- Arachnoid cap cells are meningeal, not cortical neurons.")

add_q("inhouse_216", CAT7,
"A 62-year-old man presents following an ischemic stroke. With his eyes closed, the physician places a familiar object (a metal key, then a quarter coin) into his right hand and asks him to identify it by touch alone. He can feel the object, describes it as cold, hard, and smooth, but has no idea what it is. When he opens his eyes, he immediately recognizes it. Basic light touch, temperature, and joint position senses in his right hand are completely normal. What is this clinical deficit called, and where is the lesion located?",
["Astereognosis (tactile agnosia); lesion in the contralateral (left) parietal association cortex (superior parietal lobule, Brodmann areas 5 and 7)", "Visual agnosia; lesion in the occipitotemporal cortex", "Prosopagnosia; lesion in the fusiform gyrus", "Anosognosia; lesion in the non-dominant frontal lobe", "Autotopagnosia; lesion in the dominant temporal lobe"],
0,
"ASTEREOGNOSIS (tactile agnosia) is the inability to recognize and identify a three-dimensional object by touch and tactile manipulation alone, in the presence of INTACT PRIMARY SENSORY MODALITIES (light touch, pain, temperature, and vibration are all preserved). It represents a high-order cortical sensory deficit caused by damage to the CONTRALATERAL PARIETAL ASSOCIATION CORTEX (specifically the superior parietal lobule, Brodmann areas 5 and 7). Primary somatosensory cortex (S1) perceives basic elemental sensations, while the parietal association cortex synthesizes these features into an integrated cognitive tactile percept.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Visual agnosia is inability to recognize objects visually, but this patient recognized the key when he opened his eyes.\n- Prosopagnosia is inability to recognize familiar human faces (fusiform gyrus lesion).\n- Anosognosia is denial of illness/deficit (non-dominant parietal lesion).\n- Autotopagnosia is inability to localize body parts.")

add_q("inhouse_217", CAT7,
"During a bedside cortical sensory examination, a neurologist asks a patient to close her eyes. Using a dull wooden applicator, the physician gently traces the number '8' on the palm of her left hand and asks her to identify the number. She is unable to recognize any numbers or letters drawn on her left palm, but correctly identifies them on her right palm. What is this sensory deficit called, and what neural structure is damaged?",
["Agraphesthesia; indicates a lesion in the contralateral (right) primary somatosensory cortex or parietal association cortex", "Astereognosis; indicates an anterior spinal cord transection", "Alexia; indicates an optic chiasm tumor", "Acalculia; indicates a frontal lobe contusion", "Apraxia; indicates a cerebellar vermis stroke"],
0,
"AGRAPHESTHESIA is the inability to recognize letters, numbers, or geometric symbols traced onto the skin (usually tested on the palm of the hand), in the presence of intact primary cutaneous touch sensation. It is a sensitive clinical sign of a cortical sensory deficit localized to the CONTRALATERAL PRIMARY SOMATOSENSORY CORTEX or adjacent parietal association cortex. Along with astereognosis and impaired two-point discrimination, agraphesthesia helps clinicians localize a lesion to the cerebral cortex rather than peripheral nerves or spinal cord.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Astereognosis is inability to identify physical 3D objects (keys, coins) by touch.\n- Alexia is inability to read written words visually.\n- Acalculia is inability to perform mathematical calculations (Gerstmann syndrome).\n- Apraxia is inability to execute learned motor tasks despite intact motor/sensory function.")

add_q("inhouse_218", CAT7,
"A clinical researcher measures two-point discrimination thresholds across different anatomical body regions using a calibrated sensory caliper. The threshold on the index fingertip is 2 to 3 mm, while the threshold on the patient's mid-back is 45 mm. Which peripheral and central factors account for the extraordinarily fine two-point discrimination on the fingertips?",
["High density of Merkel and Meissner receptors with tiny receptive fields, combined with large dedicated cortical magnification in the primary somatosensory cortex and strong lateral inhibition", "Low density of receptors and large overlapping receptive fields", "Exclusive innervation by unmyelinated C-fibers", "Direct axonal projection to the motor cortex bypassing the thalamus", "Thick stratum corneum that focuses pressure waves into single axons"],
0,
"Two-point discrimination is the minimum physical distance at which two simultaneous tactile stimuli can be perceived as distinct, separate points rather than a single point. Fine spatial acuity on the fingertips (2-3 mm) and lips is achieved through multiple integrated mechanisms:\n1) Peripheral: Massive receptor density of Merkel discs and Meissner corpuscles, each having extremely SMALL RECEPTIVE FIELDS (2-4 mm) with minimal overlap;\n2) Central: Large CORTICAL MAGNIFICATION in the S1 homunculus (thousands of dedicated cortical columns per cm2 of fingertip skin);\n3) Synaptic: Strong LATERAL INHIBITION mediated by GABAergic interneurons in the dorsal column nuclei and cortex, which sharpens spatial borders by suppressing flanking signals from adjacent receptors.\nOn the back, receptor density is sparse, receptive fields are huge (>40 mm), and cortical representation is minimal, resulting in poor two-point resolution (threshold 40-50 mm).\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Low receptor density and large fields describe the back, not the fingertips.\n- Tactile discrimination is mediated by heavily myelinated A-beta fibers, not C-fibers.\n- Sensory projections strictly traverse the VPL thalamus before reaching cortex.\n- Stratum corneum does not focus pressure waves into single axons.")

add_q("inhouse_219", CAT7,
"A 68-year-old woman recovering from a right middle cerebral artery stroke is tested for cortical sensory extinction. When the physician touches her right hand alone, she accurately perceives it. When the physician touches her left hand alone, she accurately perceives it. However, when the physician touches BOTH hands simultaneously, she reports feeling the touch ONLY on her right hand, completely failing to perceive the touch on her left hand. What is this phenomenon called, and which lobe is damaged?",
["Sensory extinction (tactile extinction on double simultaneous stimulation); lesion in the non-dominant (right) parietal lobe", "Bilateral peripheral neuropathy; lesion in the stocking-glove distribution", "Hemi-inattention due to bilateral occipital lobe infarction", "Sensory allodynia due to dorsal horn hyperalgesia", "Prosopagnosia due to fusiform gyrus damage"],
0,
"SENSORY EXTINCTION (tactile extinction on double simultaneous stimulation) is a hallmark cortical sensory deficit typically associated with a lesion in the NON-DOMINANT (usually right) PARIETAL LOBE (specifically the inferior parietal lobule / temporoparietal junction). When stimuli are presented in isolation to either side, primary sensory pathways are intact and the patient perceives the touch normally. However, when bilateral simultaneous stimuli compete for conscious attentional processing, the intact dominant hemisphere dominates, and the damaged non-dominant parietal cortex fails to attend to the contralateral (left) stimulus, causing it to be 'extinguished' from conscious awareness.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Peripheral neuropathy would impair single stimuli in a length-dependent pattern.\n- Occipital lesions affect visual fields, not somatosensory tactile extinction.\n- Allodynia is pain evoked by non-noxious stimuli.\n- Prosopagnosia is facial recognition agnosia.")

add_q("inhouse_220", CAT7,
"A 54-year-old man presents with progressive unsteadiness when walking, especially at night when walking down a dark hallway or when closing his eyes in the shower. Physical examination reveals that when he stands with his feet together and eyes open, he is steady. However, when he closes his eyes, he immediately begins swaying violently and loses his balance, requiring the physician to catch him (positive Romberg test). What sensory pathway is impaired, and why does his unsteadiness appear only when his eyes are closed?",
["Dorsal Column-Medial Lemniscal (DCML) conscious proprioceptive pathway; maintaining balance requires 2 of 3 sensory inputs (proprioception, vision, vestibular); when proprioception is lost, vision compensates, but closing the eyes removes visual compensation, unmasking sensory ataxia", "Cerebellar vermis; the cerebellum operates exclusively in the dark", "Lateral spinothalamic tract; temperature sensation is required for balance", "Vestibulocochlear nerve; auditory input stabilizes the head", "Corticospinal tract; motor neurons are paralyzed by darkness"],
0,
"Maintaining upright postural equilibrium requires the central nervous system to receive sensory input from AT LEAST TWO of the following THREE modalities:\n1) PROPRIOCEPTION (joint position sense via DCML pathways);\n2) VISION;\n3) VESTIBULAR FUNCTION.\nA POSITIVE ROMBERG TEST is defined as the ability to maintain balance with feet together while eyes are open, followed by unsteadiness, marked swaying, or falling when the eyes are closed. This indicates SENSORY ATAXIA due to severe loss of PROPRIOCEPTION in the dorsal columns (e.g., tabes dorsalis, vitamin B12 deficiency, sensory neuronopathy). With eyes open, visual compensation maintains balance; closing the eyes removes visual input, leaving only the vestibular system, which is insufficient, unmasking the profound sensory ataxia. (In contrast, CEREBELLAR ATAXIA causes unsteadiness with eyes open AND closed; Romberg is negative).\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Cerebellar ataxia causes unsteadiness even with eyes open.\n- Spinothalamic pain/temp tracts do not mediate postural balance.\n- Vestibulocochlear auditory signals do not maintain posture.\n- Corticospinal tracts are motor pathways.")

add_q("inhouse_221", CAT7,
"A 35-year-old woman with multiple sclerosis develops an isolated demyelinating plaque localized to the left dorsal column at the C4 spinal cord level. What pattern of sensory loss will this patient demonstrate on physical examination below the C4 dermatome?",
["IPSILATERAL loss of vibration, joint position sense, and fine discriminative touch below C4, with completely normal pain and temperature sensation bilaterally", "CONTRALATERAL loss of vibration and proprioception below C4", "Bilateral loss of pain and temperature sensation across the shoulders", "Ipsilateral loss of pain and temperature below C4", "Complete bilateral sensory loss to all modalities below C4"],
0,
"Primary sensory axons of the DCML pathway enter the spinal cord via the dorsal roots and ascend IPSILATERALLY in the dorsal columns (fasciculus gracilis and cuneatus) WITHOUT CROSSING THE MIDLINE until they reach the second-order nuclei in the caudal medulla. Therefore, a unilateral lesion of the dorsal column in the spinal cord (e.g., an MS plaque, tumor, or stab wound at left C4) damages ascending uncrossed fibers, producing IPSILATERAL LOSS OF VIBRATION, PROPRIOCEPTION, AND FINE DISCRIMINATIVE TOUCH below the level of the lesion (from C4 down on the left side). Pain and temperature are carried by the spinothalamic tract in the anterolateral funiculus and are completely spared.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Contralateral DCML loss occurs only with lesions rostral to the sensory decussation (in the brainstem, thalamus, or cortex).\n- Bilateral pain/temp loss describes syringomyelia.\n- Ipsilateral pain/temp loss does not occur with isolated dorsal column lesions.\n- Complete bilateral loss describes complete spinal cord transection.")

add_q("inhouse_222", CAT7,
"A 63-year-old man suffers an ischemic stroke resulting from occlusion of a paramedian branch of the basilar artery, causing a localized infarction of the right Medial Lemniscus in the mid-pons. What sensory deficits will be present on physical examination?",
["CONTRALATERAL (left-sided) loss of vibration, joint position sense, and fine touch across the left arm, trunk, and leg", "IPSILATERAL (right-sided) loss of vibration and joint position sense", "Contralateral loss of pain and temperature with intact proprioception", "Bilateral loss of all sensation in a cape-like distribution", "Isolated facial numbness with normal limb sensation"],
0,
"The internal arcuate fibers of the DCML pathway decussate in the caudal medulla to form the Medial Lemniscus. Consequently, above the level of the medullary decussation, the Medial Lemniscus in the pons and midbrain carries sensory information from the OPPOSITE side of the body. An isolated infarction of the RIGHT Medial Lemniscus in the pons damages ascending second-order fibers representing the left side of the body, producing CONTRALATERAL (LEFT-SIDED) LOSS OF VIBRATION, PROPRIOCEPTION, AND FINE DISCRIMINATIVE TOUCH across the left limbs and trunk. Pain and temperature (anterolateral system, which runs more laterally in the pontine tegmentum) and facial sensation may be spared if the infarct is small and paramedian.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Ipsilateral loss occurs only with spinal cord dorsal column lesions, below the decussation.\n- Contralateral pain/temp loss occurs with spinothalamic tract lesions.\n- Cape-like distribution describes cervical syringomyelia.\n- Isolated facial numbness occurs with trigeminal sensory nucleus lesions.")

add_q("inhouse_223", CAT7,
"A 70-year-old hypertensive diabetic man presents with sudden, complete numbness involving his entire left side. Neurological examination reveals complete loss of pain, temperature, light touch, vibration, and proprioception affecting the left face, left arm, left trunk, and left leg equally. Motor strength is completely 5/5 in all muscle groups, visual fields are full, and speech and cognition are normal. A brain MRI reveals a 1 cm lacunar infarct in the thalamus. Which thalamic nuclei are infarcted to produce this pure sensory stroke, and which arterial branches are occluded?",
["Ventral Posterolateral (VPL) and Ventral Posteromedial (VPM) nuclei of the thalamus; occluding thalamoperforating / thalamogeniculate branches of the Posterior Cerebral Artery (PCA)", "Lateral Geniculate Nucleus; occluding anterior choroidal artery", "Ventral Lateral nucleus; occluding lenticulostriate branches of the MCA", "Anterior thalamic nucleus; occluding anterior cerebral artery", "Centromedian nucleus; occluding superior cerebellar artery"],
0,
"A classic PURE SENSORY STROKE is one of the classic lacunar syndromes caused by microatheroma or lipohyalinosis of penetrating THALAMOGENICULATE / THALAMOPERFORATING BRANCHES of the POSTERIOR CEREBRAL ARTERY (PCA). The infarction is localized to the primary somatosensory relay nuclei of the contralateral thalamus:\n- VENTRAL POSTEROLATERAL (VPL) NUCLEUS: relays all sensory modalities (pain, temp, touch, vibration, proprioception) from the body;\n- VENTRAL POSTEROMEDIAL (VPM) NUCLEUS: relays all sensory modalities from the face (trigeminal lemniscus).\nInfarction of both nuclei abolishes all sensation across the entire contralateral hemibody (face, arm, trunk, leg). Because motor pathways in the internal capsule and cortex are spared, motor strength is 5/5 and no cortical signs (aphasia, neglect, visual field defects) are present.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- LGN infarction causes contralateral homonymous hemianopia.\n- Ventral Lateral nucleus mediates cerebellar motor circuits.\n- Anterior thalamic nucleus is part of the limbic Papez circuit (memory/emotion).\n- Centromedian nucleus mediates arousal and basal ganglia loops.")

add_q("inhouse_224", CAT7,
"Six months after sustaining the thalamic lacunar stroke described above, the patient returns to clinic complaining of agonizing, burning, and searing pain across his left arm, trunk, and leg. Even the light touch of clothing or a gentle breeze against his left arm provokes excruciating, intolerable pain (severe allodynia and hyperpathia). Neurological examination reveals persistent sensory loss to pinprick and vibration, yet the spontaneous burning pain is unbearable. What post-stroke syndrome has developed?",
["Dejerine-Roussy syndrome (Thalamic Pain Syndrome / Central Post-Stroke Pain)", "Complex Regional Pain Syndrome type 1", "Trigeminal neuralgia", "Tabes dorsalis", "Phantom limb syndrome"],
0,
"DEJERINE-ROUSSY SYNDROME (Thalamic Pain Syndrome, or Central Post-Stroke Pain) is a debilitating neuropathic pain syndrome that develops in approximately 5% to 10% of patients following an ischemic or hemorrhagic stroke involving the sensory nuclei of the thalamus (VPL/VPM). Typically developing weeks to months after the initial stroke, the initial hemisensory loss is replaced by severe, intractable, spontaneous burning, freezing, or lancinating pain in the contralateral hemibody. Patients display marked ALLODYNIA (pain provoked by innocuous stimuli like clothing or touch) and HYPERPATHIA. The pathophysiology involves central sensitization and loss of thalamic GABAergic gating/inhibition of pain pathways, leading to runaway cortical pain signaling. The pain is notoriously refractory to standard analgesics and opioids; first-line therapies include gabapentinoids, tricyclic antidepressants (amitriptyline), or SNRIs.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- CRPS type 1 follows peripheral trauma or fractures in an extremity, not a cerebral stroke.\n- Trigeminal neuralgia causes paroxysmal brief facial pain, not hemibody burning pain.\n- Tabes dorsalis is neurosyphilitic dorsal column degeneration.\n- Phantom limb requires prior physical amputation.")

add_q("inhouse_225", CAT7,
"During a major corrective spinal deformity surgery (scoliosis spinal fusion), neurophysiologists continuously record Somatosensory Evoked Potentials (SSEPs) to monitor spinal cord integrity. Electrical stimulation of the posterior tibial nerve at the ankle generates ascending volleys recorded over the scalp as the P37/N45 cortical waveform. Which spinal cord pathway is primarily being monitored by SSEPs?",
["Dorsal columns (fasciculus gracilis)", "Lateral spinothalamic tract", "Lateral corticospinal tract", "Anterior spinocerebellar tract", "Anterior white commissure"],
0,
"Somatosensory Evoked Potentials (SSEPs) are the standard intraoperative neurophysiological monitoring modality used during spine surgery to detect impending spinal cord ischemia or mechanical traction injury. When a peripheral nerve is stimulated (e.g., posterior tibial nerve at the ankle for lower extremities, or median nerve at the wrist for upper extremities), the electrical signal travels via large, myelinated A-beta fibers that ascend exclusively through the DORSAL COLUMNS of the spinal cord (fasciculus gracilis for tibial nerve, fasciculus cuneatus for median nerve) to the medulla, thalamus, and primary somatosensory cortex. A significant drop in SSEP amplitude (>50%) or increase in latency (>10%) warns the surgeon of compromised dorsal column perfusion (supplied by posterior spinal arteries) or spinal cord stretch, allowing immediate corrective action to prevent permanent paraplegia.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Spinothalamic tract is unmyelinated/thinly myelinated and does not generate the synchronized high-amplitude waveforms of SSEPs.\n- Lateral corticospinal tract is monitored by Motor Evoked Potentials (MEPs via transcranial electrical stimulation).\n- Spinocerebellar tracts terminate in the cerebellum, not the primary somatosensory cortex.\n- Anterior white commissure carries crossing pain fibers.")


# Q226 - Q245
add_q("inhouse_226", CAT8,
"A 40-year-old construction worker accidentally touches a live electrical wire, sustaining a painful jolt, and simultaneously burns his hand on a hot engine pipe. He accurately localizes the sharp shock, feels the throbbing heat, and experiences intense emotional distress. What are the cardinal sensory modalities transmitted by the Anterolateral System (ALS / Spinothalamic tract)?",
["Nociception (pain), thermal sensation (heat and cold), crude (non-discriminative) touch, and itch", "Fine two-point discrimination, vibration, and conscious proprioception", "Unconscious proprioception to the cerebellum exclusively", "Static joint position sense and stereognosis exclusively", "Somatic motor control of axial trunk muscles"],
0,
"The Anterolateral System (ALS, encompassing the spinothalamic, spinoreticular, spinomesencephalic, and spinohypothalamic tracts) is the primary ascending sensory system carrying protopathic sensations. Its cardinal modalities include:\n1) NOCICEPTION (pain from mechanical, thermal, and chemical tissue damage);\n2) TEMPERATURE (warmth mediated by C-fibers, cold mediated by A-delta fibers);\n3) CRUDE TOUCH (poorly localized, non-discriminative tactile pressure);\n4) ITCH (pruritus mediated by histamine- and non-histamine-sensitive C-fibers);\n5) Tickle and sexual sensations.\nIn contrast, epicritic sensations (fine touch, vibration, conscious proprioception, stereognosis) are carried by the DCML pathway.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Two-point discrimination, vibration, and conscious proprioception are carried by the DCML pathway.\n- Unconscious proprioception is carried by the spinocerebellar tracts.\n- Stereognosis is mediated by DCML and parietal cortex.\n- Somatic motor control is mediated by corticospinal tracts.")

add_q("inhouse_227", CAT8,
"A neurobiologist presenting at a pain symposium emphasizes the fundamental physiological distinction between 'nociception' and 'pain.' Which statement accurately captures this distinction according to the International Association for the Study of Pain (IASP)?",
["Nociception is the physiological detection, transduction, and neural encoding of noxious stimuli by sensory receptors; Pain is the subjective, conscious, unpleasant sensory and emotional experience", "Nociception is conscious emotional suffering; Pain is the activation of peripheral free nerve endings", "Nociception occurs only in the brainstem; Pain occurs only in peripheral unmyelinated axons", "Nociception is abolished by local anesthetics, whereas pain is unaffected by nerve blocks", "There is no physiological or psychological distinction between nociception and pain"],
0,
"The IASP strictly differentiates nociception from pain:\n- NOCICEPTION is the objective, physiological sensory process: the biological detection, transduction, and encoding of noxious (tissue-damaging) mechanical, thermal, or chemical stimuli by peripheral nociceptors and its transmission along peripheral axons and spinal pathways. Nociception can occur in an unconscious, anesthetized patient (triggering reflex tachycardia or withdrawal);\n- PAIN is an intensely subjective, conscious cortical experience: an unpleasant sensory and emotional experience associated with, or resembling that associated with, actual or potential tissue damage. Pain requires cortical processing (S1, S2, anterior cingulate cortex, insula) and is shaped by emotion, cognition, culture, and memory.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Nociception is the objective neural encoding; pain is the conscious emotional experience.\n- Nociception begins in the periphery; pain is realized cortically.\n- Both are modulated by local anesthetics.\n- The distinction is fundamental to clinical neurology and anesthesiology.")

add_q("inhouse_228", CAT8,
"When a person accidentally stubs their bare toe against a hard concrete step, they instantly feel a sharp, localized, pricking pain ('first pain'), followed 1 to 2 seconds later by a prolonged, poorly localized, throbbing, burning ache ('second pain'). Which nerve fiber types conduct 'first pain' and 'second pain,' respectively?",
["First pain is conducted by thinly myelinated A-delta fibers (5-30 m/s); Second pain is conducted by unmyelinated C-fibers (0.5-2 m/s)", "First pain is conducted by unmyelinated C-fibers; Second pain is conducted by heavily myelinated A-alpha fibers", "First pain is conducted by A-beta fibers; Second pain is conducted by Group Ia fibers", "First pain is conducted by B-fibers; Second pain is conducted by A-delta fibers", "Both first and second pain are conducted simultaneously by A-alpha fibers"],
0,
"The dual perception of pain following acute mechanical injury reflects transmission along two distinct primary afferent fiber populations:\n1) 'FIRST PAIN' (epicritic fast pain): Conducted by THINLY MYELINATED A-DELTA FIBERS (axon diameter 1-5 um, conduction velocity 5-30 m/s). It is perceived virtually instantaneously as a sharp, pricking, well-localized sensation that triggers rapid reflex withdrawal;\n2) 'SECOND PAIN' (protopathic slow pain): Conducted by SLOW, UNMYELINATED C-FIBERS (axon diameter 0.2-1.5 um, conduction velocity 0.5-2 m/s). Due to their slow conduction, the signal arrives 1 to 2 seconds later and is perceived as a diffuse, poorly localized, dull, aching, or burning sensation that persists long after the stimulus ends.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- C-fibers conduct second pain, not first pain.\n- A-beta fibers conduct fine touch, not acute pain.\n- B-fibers are preganglionic autonomic fibers.\n- A-alpha fibers are proprioceptive and motor fibers.")

add_q("inhouse_229", CAT8,
"Primary afferent nociceptive fibers (A-delta and C fibers) enter the spinal cord through the lateral division of the dorsal root entry zone. Before synapsing on second-order neurons in the dorsal horn gray matter, these axons branch and travel longitudinally in which tract?",
["Tract of Lissauer (dorsolateral fasciculus)", "Fasciculus gracilis", "Anterior white commissure", "Rubrospinal tract", "Lateral corticospinal tract"],
0,
"Upon entering the spinal cord, primary pain and temperature afferents (A-delta and C fibers) do not immediately terminate; their axons enter the TRACT OF LISSAUER (dorsolateral fasciculus or dorsolateral tract of Lissauer), located at the apex of the dorsal horn. Within Lissauer tract, these fibers branch and ASCEND OR DESCEND 1 TO 2 SPINAL CORD SEGMENTS before penetrating the dorsal horn gray matter to synapse in Rexed laminae I (marginal zone), II (substantia gelatinosa), and V. This intersegmental spread explains why cord lesions cause contralateral pain and temperature loss that begins 1 to 2 dermatomes BELOW the anatomical level of the lesion.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Fasciculus gracilis carries uncrossed DCML touch/proprioception from the lower body.\n- Anterior white commissure carries decussating second-order spinothalamic axons.\n- Rubrospinal tract is a descending motor pathway.\n- Lateral corticospinal tract is a descending motor pathway.")

add_q("inhouse_230", CAT8,
"Where do the second-order axons of the Anterolateral System (spinothalamic tract) decussate across the midline of the central nervous system, and within how many spinal cord segments from their entry level does this decussation occur?",
["In the ANTERIOR WHITE COMMISSURE of the spinal cord, within 1 to 2 segments of the primary entry level", "In the sensory decussation of the caudal medulla, 10 segments above entry", "In the optic chiasm, within the diencephalon", "In the posterior commissure of the midbrain", "They do not decussate; spinothalamic fibers ascend strictly uncrossed"],
0,
"The second-order neurons of the spinothalamic tract have cell bodies in Rexed laminae I and V of the spinal cord dorsal horn. Their axons DECUSSATE IMMEDIATELY ACROSS THE VENTRAL MIDLINE in the ANTERIOR WHITE COMMISSURE (located immediately ventral to the central canal) within 1 to 2 spinal cord segments of entry. Once across the midline, the fibers gather in the contralateral anterolateral funiculus and ascend all the way to the thalamus and brainstem. (This contrasts sharply with the DCML pathway, which ascends uncrossed in the spinal cord and decussates in the medulla).\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Sensory decussation in the caudal medulla is the crossing site of the DCML pathway (internal arcuate fibers), not spinothalamic tract.\n- Optic chiasm decussates retinal ganglion cell axons.\n- Posterior commissure mediates pupillary light reflexes.\n- Spinothalamic fibers do decussate; that is why unilateral cord lesions produce contralateral pain/temp loss.")

add_q("inhouse_231", CAT8,
"The ascending spinothalamic tract within the anterolateral funiculus is somatotopically laminated. As new fibers from rostral spinal cord segments decussate and enter the tract, how are they layered relative to older fibers that entered from caudal segments?",
["Fibers from sacral segments are located POSTEROLATERALLY (superficially), while fibers from cervical segments are located ANTEROMEDIALLY (deep)", "Fibers from sacral segments are located anteromedially, while cervical fibers are posterolateral", "Fibers from the arms are mixed randomly with fibers from the legs", "Fibers from sacral segments ascend in the dorsal horn, while cervical fibers ascend in the ventral horn", "Lower extremity fibers are exclusively uncrossed, while upper extremity fibers are crossed"],
0,
"The spinothalamic tract exhibits precise somatotopic laminar organization within the anterolateral funiculus:\n- As axons decussate at sacral and lumbar levels, they occupy the most peripheral, superficial rim;\n- As the tract ascends, newly entering second-order axons from thoracic and cervical levels are added to the MEDIAL and DEEP aspect of the tract;\n- Consequently, SACRAL AND LUMBAR FIBERS LIE POSTEROLATERALLY (superficially near the surface of the cord), while CERVICAL FIBERS LIE ANTEROMEDIALLY (deep near the anterior horn). (Mnemonic: 'Sacral is Superficial / Lateral').\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Sacral is lateral/superficial, not anteromedial/deep.\n- Fibers are highly somatotopically organized, not randomly mixed.\n- Both upper and lower fibers ascend in the anterolateral funiculus.\n- All spinothalamic fibers decussate.")

add_q("inhouse_232", CAT8,
"A 45-year-old woman develops an extramedullary meningioma growing from the anterolateral spinal dura at the C5 level, which slowly compresses the surface of the left anterolateral spinal cord. Due to the somatotopic lamination of the spinothalamic tract, what pattern of sensory deficit develops first?",
["Loss of pain and temperature sensation beginning in the contralateral sacral/perineal region that ascends upward over months", "Loss of pain and temperature sensation beginning in the ipsilateral neck and arm", "Isolated loss of proprioception in the fingers with sparing of the legs", "Immediate bilateral loss of all sensation in the hands exclusively", "Bilateral loss of vibration sense in the toes"],
0,
"Because sacral and lumbar fibers of the spinothalamic tract are located POSTEROLATERALLY on the superficial surface of the spinal cord, an EXTRAMEDULLARY MASS (such as a meningioma, schwannoma, or metastatic tumor compressing the spinal cord from the outside) compresses the superficial SACRAL FIBERS FIRST. This produces contralateral loss of pain and temperature that begins in the SADDLE / SACRAL DERMATOMES and ASCENDS UPWARD over time as deeper fibers are gradually compressed. In contrast, an INTRAMEDULLARY MASS (such as syringomyelia or an ependymoma expanding from the central canal) compresses deep cervical fibers first, producing descending sensory loss with 'SACRAL SPARING'.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Extramedullary compression affects contralateral sacral fibers first, not ipsilateral neck/arm.\n- Proprioception is dorsal column, not spinothalamic.\n- Isolated hand loss with sacral sparing describes intramedullary lesions (syringomyelia).\n- Vibration sense is dorsal column.")

add_q("inhouse_233", CAT8,
"The Anterolateral System does not project solely to the primary somatosensory cortex; it distributes collateral axons to several brainstem and limbic targets. Which tract of the ALS projects to the midbrain to trigger endogenous descending analgesia, and which tract projects to the limbic system to mediate the emotional suffering of pain?",
["Endogenous analgesia: Spinomesencephalic tract (projects to Periaqueductal Gray); Emotional suffering: Spinolimbic tract (projects to anterior cingulate and insular cortices)", "Endogenous analgesia: Corticospinal tract; Emotional suffering: Dorsal column", "Endogenous analgesia: Spinocerebellar tract; Emotional suffering: Medial lemniscus", "Endogenous analgesia: Tectospinal tract; Emotional suffering: Rubrospinal tract", "Endogenous analgesia: Vestibulospinal tract; Emotional suffering: Olivocerebellar tract"],
0,
"The Anterolateral System is composed of multiple functional ascending pathways:\n1) SPINOTHALAMIC TRACT: Projects to the VPL thalamus and S1/S2 cortex; mediates the sensory-discriminative aspects of pain (exact location, intensity, sharp vs dull);\n2) SPINOMESENCEPHALIC TRACT: Projects to the PERIAQUEDUCTAL GRAY (PAG) and superior colliculus of the midbrain; activates the descending endogenous opioid analgesia pathway to suppress spinal pain transmission;\n3) SPINORETICULAR TRACT: Projects to the brainstem reticular formation and intralaminar thalamic nuclei; mediates autonomic arousal, alertness, and insomnia caused by pain;\n4) SPINOLIMBIC & SPINOHYPOTHALAMIC TRACTS: Project to the amygdala, hypothalamus, ANTERIOR CINGULATE CORTEX, and INSULA; mediate the affective-motivational, emotional distress, and suffering of pain.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Corticospinal tract is a descending motor pathway.\n- Spinocerebellar and medial lemniscus do not mediate pain or analgesia.\n- Tectospinal and rubrospinal tracts are descending motor pathways.\n- Vestibulospinal and olivocerebellar pathways mediate balance and cerebellar learning.")

add_q("inhouse_234", CAT8,
"A soldier wounded in combat feels surprisingly little pain from a severe shrapnel laceration until hours later when he reaches safety. This phenomenon of stress-induced analgesia is mediated by the descending endogenous pain modulation pathway. What is the anatomical sequence of this descending analgesic circuit?",
["Periaqueductal Gray (PAG) in the midbrain -> Rostral Ventromedial Medulla (Nucleus Raphe Magnus) -> Descending serotonergic and noradrenergic fibers -> Enkephalinergic interneurons in the spinal cord dorsal horn", "Primary motor cortex -> Red nucleus -> Ventral horn alpha motor neurons", "Cerebellar cortex -> Fastigial nucleus -> Vestibular nuclei", "Hypothalamus -> Posterior pituitary -> Systemic oxytocin release", "Hippocampus -> Fornix -> Mammillary bodies"],
0,
"The descending pain suppression pathway is the primary endogenous analgesia system of the central nervous system:\n1) Cortical, limbic, and spinomesencephalic inputs activate neurons in the PERIAQUEDUCTAL GRAY (PAG) of the midbrain;\n2) PAG neurons project to the ROSTRAL VENTROMEDIAL MEDULLA (RVM), specifically the NUCLEUS RAPHE MAGNUS (serotonergic) and the locus coeruleus (noradrenergic);\n3) Serotonergic and noradrenergic axons descend in the dorsolateral funiculus of the spinal cord to the dorsal horn (substantia gelatinosa, lamina II);\n4) They synapse on inhibitory ENKEPHALINERGIC INTERNEURONS, which release enkephalin (an endogenous opioid). Enkephalin binds presynaptic and postsynaptic mu-opioid receptors: it closes presynaptic Ca2+ channels (blocking substance P and glutamate release) and opens postsynaptic K+ channels (hyperpolarizing 2nd-order spinothalamic neurons), profoundly suppressing pain transmission.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Primary motor cortex to red nucleus is the rubrospinal pathway, not pain suppression.\n- Cerebellar fastigial circuitry controls axial balance.\n- Hypothalamus to posterior pituitary releases ADH and oxytocin.\n- Hippocampus through fornix is the Papez memory circuit.")

add_q("inhouse_235", CAT8,
"When a person steps barefoot onto a sharp rusty tack, their foot is instantly pulled upward away from the floor (flexion withdrawal reflex), while their opposite leg simultaneously extends rigidly to prevent falling (crossed extensor reflex). What neural circuitry in the spinal cord coordinates this bilateral protective behavior?",
["Polysynaptic spinal reflex: A-delta nociceptive afferents activate excitatory interneurons that stimulate ipsilateral flexors and inhibitory interneurons that relax ipsilateral extensors (reciprocal inhibition), while commissural interneurons excite contralateral extensors and inhibit contralateral flexors", "Monosynaptic reflex mediated exclusively by Group Ia muscle spindle afferents", "Direct monosynaptic activation of the motor cortex via the corticospinal tract", "Purely peripheral axon reflex mediated by local substance P release", "Autogenic inhibition mediated by Golgi tendon organs"],
0,
"The withdrawal reflex (flexor withdrawal reflex) coupled with the crossed extensor reflex is a protective, POLYSYNAPTIC SPINAL REFLEX:\n1) Noxious stimulation excites A-delta and C nociceptors, which enter the dorsal horn;\n2) In the ipsilateral spinal cord, excitatory interneurons activate alpha motor neurons to FLEXOR muscles (hamstrings, hip flexors), pulling the limb away, while inhibitory interneurons relax EXTENSOR muscles (quadriceps) through RECIPROCAL INHIBITION;\n3) Simultaneously, commissural interneurons send axons across the anterior white commissure to the contralateral ventral horn: they EXCITE CONTRALATERAL EXTENSORS and INHIBIT CONTRALATERAL FLEXORS (CROSSED EXTENSOR REFLEX), stiffening the opposite limb to bear the entire body weight and maintain upright balance.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Monosynaptic reflex describes deep tendon reflexes (patellar reflex), not withdrawal.\n- Cortical processing is not required; this reflex occurs at the spinal cord level even in spinal transection.\n- The axon reflex is local flare/vasodilation, not coordinated limb flexion.\n- GTOs mediate autogenic relaxation during high tension, not withdrawal from pain.")

add_q("inhouse_236", CAT8,
"A 28-year-old man survives a knife stabbing that penetrates the right side of his thoracic spinal cord at the T10 level, resulting in a complete hemisection of the right half of the spinal cord (Brown-Séquard syndrome). Neurological examination below the level of the lesion will reveal which of the following combinations of deficits?",
["Ipsilateral loss of vibration and proprioception, ipsilateral spastic paralysis, and CONTRALATERAL loss of pain and temperature sensation beginning at T12", "Contralateral loss of vibration and proprioception, contralateral spastic paralysis, and ipsilateral loss of pain and temperature", "Bilateral loss of pain and temperature with preserved motor strength", "Ipsilateral loss of pain and temperature, with contralateral loss of vibration and motor strength", "Complete bilateral loss of all motor and sensory modalities below T10"],
0,
"Brown-Séquard syndrome is spinal cord HEMISECTION (e.g., stab wound or lateral compression). In a right T10 hemisection, the anatomical consequences below the lesion are:\n1) IPSILATERAL loss of fine touch, vibration, and conscious proprioception below T10 (due to transection of the uncrossed right dorsal columns);\n2) IPSILATERAL upper motor neuron spastic paralysis below T10 (due to transection of the uncrossed right lateral corticospinal tract, with hyperreflexia and positive Babinski);\n3) CONTRALATERAL loss of pain and temperature sensation beginning 1 to 2 dermatomes below the lesion (at T12 and below, due to transection of the crossed spinothalamic tract carrying fibers from the left side of the body);\n4) At the exact level of T10 on the right: complete flaccid LMN paralysis and anesthesia in the T10 dermatome/myotome.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Contralateral dorsal column and motor loss is incorrect; these pathways cross in the medulla, so cord lesions cause IPSILATERAL deficits.\n- Bilateral pain/temp loss describes syringomyelia.\n- Ipsilateral pain/temp with contralateral motor loss is the exact opposite of reality.\n- Complete bilateral loss describes complete cord transection.")

add_q("inhouse_237", CAT8,
"In the patient with right-sided T10 spinal cord hemisection (Brown-Séquard syndrome), why does the contralateral loss of pain and temperature sensation begin at the T12 dermatome (1 to 2 segments below the lesion) rather than at the exact T10 level?",
["Primary afferent fibers entering at T10 and T11 ascend 1 to 2 segments in the Tract of Lissauer before synapsing and decussating across the anterior white commissure", "Corticospinal fibers suppress pain transmission at the level of the lesion", "Dorsal column fibers compensate for pain sensation for two spinal levels", "The spinothalamic tract decussates in the medulla oblongata", "Spinal nerve roots exit 2 vertebrae below their corresponding cord segments"],
0,
"When the right spinothalamic tract is transected at T10, all ascending second-order fibers within it are severed. These fibers originated from the left dorsal horn and decussated across the anterior white commissure. However, primary nociceptive afferents entering the left dorsal root at levels T10 and T11 do not immediately cross: they enter the TRACT OF LISSAUER and ASCEND 1 TO 2 SPINAL CORD SEGMENTS before synapsing and crossing. Therefore, fibers entering at left T10 ascend to T8 or T9 before crossing into the right spinothalamic tract (which is ABOVE the T10 cut). Only fibers entering at left T12 and below cross at or below T10, where their ascending tract is severed. Consequently, CONTRALATERAL PAIN AND TEMPERATURE SENSATION IS INTACT DOWN TO T11, AND IS LOST AT T12 AND BELOW.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Corticospinal fibers do not suppress the sensory level.\n- Dorsal columns do not carry pain/temperature sensation.\n- Spinothalamic tract decussates in the spinal cord, not the medulla.\n- Nerve root vertebral anatomy does not explain tract ascending dynamics in Lissauer tract.")

add_q("inhouse_238", CAT8,
"A 30-year-old woman presents with severe weakness and wasting of her hands. Neurological examination reveals bilateral flaccid weakness, marked atrophy of the thenar and hypothenar eminences, and fasciculations in both hands. Sensory examination demonstrates complete loss of pain and temperature sensation in a bilateral 'cape-like' distribution across her shoulders, neck, and upper chest, with completely normal vibration and joint position sense. Cervical MRI confirms a large syringomyelia cavity at C5-T1. What structures have been destroyed by the syrinx to cause these specific motor and sensory deficits?",
["Crossing spinothalamic fibers in the anterior white commissure (causing cape-like pain/temp loss) and lower motor neurons in the anterior gray horns (causing bilateral hand wasting and fasciculations)", "Dorsal columns and lateral corticospinal tracts exclusively", "Posterior spinocerebellar tracts and nucleus cuneatus", "Sympathetic chain ganglia and dorsal root ganglia", "Ventral white commissure and medial lemniscus in the medulla"],
0,
"Syringomyelia begins as a cystic cavitation within or adjacent to the central canal of the spinal cord (most commonly C8-T1). As the syrinx expands:\n1) It first compresses and destroys decussating second-order spinothalamic fibers crossing in the ANTERIOR WHITE COMMISSURE, producing bilateral 'cape-like' loss of pain and temperature in the corresponding dermatomes, while preserving the dorsal columns (dissociated sensory loss);\n2) As it expands further ventrolaterally into the gray matter, it destroys LOWER MOTOR NEURONS IN THE ANTERIOR HORNS (lamina IX) of the lower cervical cord (C8-T1), producing flaccid weakness, severe muscle atrophy, hyporeflexia, and fasciculations in the intrinsic hand muscles (thenar, hypothenar, interossei).\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Dorsal column destruction would abolish vibration and proprioception, which are normal here.\n- Spinocerebellar tracts mediate ataxia, not hand wasting.\n- Sympathetic chain ganglia lie outside the spine in the paravertebral chain.\n- Medial lemniscus is in the brainstem, not the cervical cord.")

add_q("inhouse_239", CAT8,
"A 58-year-old man presents with a 2-year history of severe unsteadiness when walking, described as 'stamping my feet because I cannot feel the floor,' which worsens dramatically in the dark. He reports brief, severe, lancinating ('lightning-like') pains shooting through his legs and abdomen. Physical examination reveals a wide-based, slapping gait, bilateral absence of vibration and position sense in both lower extremities, absent knee and ankle reflexes, and a positive Romberg sign. When light is shone into his eyes, neither pupil constricts; however, both pupils constrict briskly when he focuses on a near object (Argyll Robertson pupils). Serum RPR and VDRL are positive. What is the diagnosis, and which spinal cord structures are degenerated?",
["Tabes dorsalis (tertiary neurosyphilis); demyelination and atrophy of the dorsal roots and dorsal columns (fasciculus gracilis and cuneatus)", "Subacute combined degeneration; demyelination of lateral corticospinal tracts alone", "Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis; degeneration of anterior horns alone", "Poliomyelitis; viral destruction of ventral horn motor neurons", "Guillain-Barré syndrome; demyelination of peripheral motor axons"],
0,
"TABES DORSALIS is a late manifestation of TERTIARY NEUROSYPHILIS caused by chronic Treponema pallidum infection. The spirochetes cause chronic obliterative endarteritis and inflammatory demyelination that selectively targets the DORSAL ROOTS (posterior nerve root entry zone) and DORSAL COLUMNS (fasciculus gracilis and cuneatus) of the spinal cord. Key hallmarks include:\n1) SENSORY ATAXIA: Loss of conscious proprioception produces a wide-based, high-stepping 'stamping' gait and a positive Romberg sign;\n2) Severe loss of vibratory, position, and discriminative tactile sense in legs;\n3) AREFLEXIA (absent DTRs due to interruption of the afferent reflex limb in dorsal roots);\n4) LANCINATING PAINS (sudden, severe, stabbing pains in legs/viscera);\n5) ARGYLL ROBERTSON PUPILS (small, irregular pupils that 'Accommodate but do not React' to light; mnemonic: ARP = Accommodation Reflex Present, Pupillary light reflex absent).\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- SCD features prominent UMN spasticity and extensor plantars, not pure areflexic dorsal root tabes.\n- ALS features combined UMN and LMN motor signs without sensory loss or Argyll Robertson pupils.\n- Polio causes asymmetric pure flaccid motor paralysis without dorsal column loss.\n- Guillain-Barré is an acute ascending post-infectious demyelinating neuropathy.")

add_q("inhouse_240", CAT8,
"A 64-year-old woman with a history of terminal ileal resection for severe Crohn disease presents with progressive numbness, paresthesias in both feet, and unsteadiness. Physical examination reveals bilateral loss of vibration and joint position sense up to the knees, sensory ataxia with a positive Romberg sign, and marked bilateral lower extremity spasticity, hyperactive 3+ patellar reflexes, and bilateral extensor plantar responses (positive Babinski sign). CBC shows macrocytic anemia (MCV 112 fL) with hypersegmented neutrophils on peripheral blood smear. Serum methylmalonic acid and homocysteine are markedly elevated. What disease is this, and which spinal cord tracts undergo demyelination?",
["Subacute Combined Degeneration (SCD) of the spinal cord due to Vitamin B12 deficiency; demyelination of the Dorsal Columns, Lateral Corticospinal Tracts, and Spinocerebellar Tracts", "Tabes dorsalis; selective degeneration of dorsal columns and dorsal roots", "Anterior spinal artery syndrome; infarction of anterior corticospinal tracts", "Brown-Séquard syndrome; hemisection of the spinal cord", "Central cord syndrome; cavitation of the central canal"],
0,
"Subacute Combined Degeneration (SCD) of the spinal cord is a classic neurodegenerative complication of VITAMIN B12 (COBALAMIN) DEFICIENCY (caused by pernicious anemia, terminal ileal resection, Crohn disease, vegan diet, gastric bypass, or chronic nitrous oxide abuse). B12 is essential for myelin maintenance via methionine synthase and methylmalonyl-CoA mutase. Vitamin B12 deficiency leads to symmetric, progressive demyelination of THREE SPECIFIC SPINAL TRACTS:\n1) DORSAL COLUMNS (fasciculus gracilis and cuneatus): causes loss of vibration, conscious proprioception, and sensory ataxia with a positive Romberg sign;\n2) LATERAL CORTICOSPINAL TRACTS: causes upper motor neuron signs: bilateral lower extremity spastic paraparesis, hyperreflexia, and extensor plantar responses (Babinski sign);\n3) SPINOCEREBELLAR TRACTS: contributes to limb and gait ataxia.\nElevated methylmalonic acid (MMA) is the definitive biochemical marker that distinguishes B12 deficiency from folate deficiency.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Tabes dorsalis features areflexia and hypotonia (LMN/root deficit), whereas SCD features spasticity and hyperreflexia (corticospinal UMN deficit).\n- Anterior spinal artery infarction spares the dorsal columns.\n- Brown-Séquard is unilateral hemisection.\n- Central cord syndrome causes cape-like weakness greater in upper extremities.")

add_q("inhouse_241", CAT8,
"A patient with severe macrocytic anemia and mild lower extremity paresthesias is treated empirically with high-dose oral folic acid (vitamin B9) without checking vitamin B12 levels. Two months later, his anemia and fatigue have completely resolved, but his paresthesias have progressed to severe spastic paraparesis and ataxia, rendering him wheelchair-bound. What explains this tragic outcome?",
["Folic acid administration corrected the megaloblastic anemia by bypassing the methyl-folate trap, but failed to treat the underlying vitamin B12 deficiency, allowing irreversible demyelination of the spinal cord to progress unabated", "Folic acid is directly neurotoxic to oligodendrocytes in high doses", "Folic acid accelerated autoimmune destruction of the dorsal horn", "Folic acid caused acute infarction of the anterior spinal artery", "Folic acid precipitated acute zinc deficiency in the basal ganglia"],
0,
"This is a classic medical board scenario illustrating the 'METHYL-FOLATE TRAP':\n- Megaloblastic anemia in vitamin B12 deficiency occurs because B12 is a required cofactor for methionine synthase (which demethylates 5-methyl-THF to generate active THF needed for thymidylate and DNA synthesis);\n- Administering large doses of FOLIC ACID (vitamin B9) bypasses this metabolic block by directly providing unmethylated folate, which successfully restores DNA synthesis and COMPLETELY RESOLVES THE MEGALOBLASTIC ANEMIA;\n- However, folic acid CANNOT replenish vitamin B12 for the second B12-dependent enzyme: METHYLMALONYL-CoA MUTASE (which converts methylmalonyl-CoA to succinyl-CoA for myelin maintenance);\n- As a result, the neurological demyelination (Subacute Combined Degeneration) ACCELERATES UNNOTICED, resulting in irreversible spastic paraparesis and ataxia. Therefore, vitamin B12 deficiency MUST ALWAYS be excluded before or treated concurrently with folic acid!\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Folic acid is not directly neurotoxic; the tragedy is unmasking/allowing B12 deficiency to progress.\n- Folic acid does not destroy the dorsal horn.\n- Folic acid does not cause ASA infarction.\n- Folic acid does not cause zinc deficiency.")

add_q("inhouse_242", CAT8,
"A 72-year-old man undergoes surgical repair of a large descending thoracoabdominal aortic aneurysm. In the recovery room, he is found to have complete flaccid paraplegia of both lower extremities and complete loss of pain and temperature sensation below the T8 level. He has acute urinary retention. Remarkably, testing of vibration sense with a 128 Hz tuning fork and joint position sense in both big toes reveals COMPLETELY INTACT, NORMAL VIBRATION AND PROPRIOCEPTION BILATERALLY. What neurovascular syndrome is present, and why are vibration and proprioception spared?",
["Anterior Spinal Artery (ASA) Syndrome; the single anterior spinal artery supplies the anterior two-thirds of the spinal cord, while the paired POSTERIOR SPINAL ARTERIES supply the dorsal columns and remain patent", "Posterior spinal artery thrombosis; the posterior spinal arteries supply motor tracts", "Brown-Séquard syndrome; spinal cord was hemisected during surgery", "Central cord syndrome; only the central canal is infarcted", "Complete spinal cord transection; all tracts are severed equally"],
0,
"Anterior Spinal Artery (ASA) syndrome (Anterior Cord Syndrome) is a classic vascular catastrophe occurring during open or endovascular repair of thoracoabdominal aortic aneurysms (due to cross-clamping of the aorta or sacrifice of intercostal/radicular arteries). The single Anterior Spinal Artery supplies the ANTERIOR TWO-THIRDS of the spinal cord. Infarction of this territory damages:\n1) Lateral and anterior corticospinal tracts: bilateral paraplegia;\n2) Spinothalamic tracts: bilateral loss of pain and temperature below the lesion;\n3) Descending autonomic micturition tracts: acute urinary retention / incontinence.\nCRUCIALLY, the DORSAL COLUMNS (fasciculus gracilis and cuneatus) occupy the posterior one-third of the spinal cord, which is independently perfused by the PAIRED POSTERIOR SPINAL ARTERIES (branches of the vertebral or PICA arteries). Consequently, VIBRATION AND JOINT POSITION SENSE ARE COMPLETELY SPARED (dissociated sensory loss).\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Posterior spinal artery thrombosis causes loss of vibration and proprioception, not motor paraplegia.\n- Brown-Séquard is a unilateral hemisection causing asymmetric deficits.\n- Central cord syndrome causes upper extremity weakness > lower extremity.\n- Complete cord transection abolishes all modalities, including vibration/proprioception.")

add_q("inhouse_243", CAT8,
"The anterior spinal artery receives crucial supplemental blood flow in the lower thoracic and upper lumbar regions from a dominant, large radicular artery. A vascular surgeon clamping the descending thoracic aorta takes great care to locate and preserve this vessel to prevent spinal cord infarction. What is the name of this major radiculomedullary feeder artery, and at which vertebral level does it most commonly arise?",
["Great radicular artery of Adamkiewicz (arteria radicularis magna); typically arises from a left posterior intercostal artery between T9 and T12", "Artery of Percheron; arises from the posterior cerebral artery", "Heubner recurrent artery; arises from the anterior cerebral artery", "Lenticulostriate artery; arises from the middle cerebral artery", "Labbe vein; arises from the transverse sinus"],
0,
"The GREAT RADICULAR ARTERY OF ADAMKIEWICZ (arteria radicularis magna) is the largest and most critical segmental radiculomedullary artery supplying the human spinal cord. In the thoracic spine, the anterior spinal artery is narrow and has poor watershed collateral perfusion. The artery of Adamkiewicz arises from a left posterior intercostal or upper lumbar artery (most commonly between T9 and T12, in ~75% of individuals on the left side) and anastomoses directly with the anterior spinal artery, providing the primary arterial supply for the lower two-thirds of the spinal cord (lower thoracic, lumbar, and sacral segments). Accidental sacrifice or cross-clamping during aortic aneurysm repair leads to immediate Anterior Spinal Artery syndrome and permanent paraplegia.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Artery of Percheron is an anatomical variant arising from the PCA that supplies bilateral paramedian thalami and midbrain.\n- Recurrent artery of Heubner arises from ACA and supplies the anterior limb of the internal capsule and caudate head.\n- Lenticulostriate arteries arise from MCA.\n- Vein of Labbe is an anastomotic cortical vein.")

add_q("inhouse_244", CAT8,
"A 76-year-old man with severe pre-existing cervical spondylosis is involved in a low-speed motor vehicle collision where his car was rear-ended, causing a violent hyperextension injury of his neck. Neurological examination reveals severe weakness, flaccidity, and loss of reflexes in both upper extremities (hands and arms strength 1/5), while his lower extremities display near-normal motor strength (4+/5) and brisk reflexes. He has patchy, cape-like loss of pain and temperature sensation across his shoulders and arms, with preserved perianal sensation (sacral sparing). What spinal cord syndrome has occurred?",
["Central Cord Syndrome", "Anterior Cord Syndrome", "Brown-Séquard syndrome", "Posterior Cord Syndrome", "Cauda Equina Syndrome"],
0,
"CENTRAL CORD SYNDROME is the most common incomplete spinal cord injury syndrome. It characteristically occurs in ELDERLY PATIENTS WITH CERVICAL SPONDYLOSIS (spinal stenosis with osteophytes) subjected to a HYPEREXTENSION INJURY of the neck. Hyperextension causes inward buckling of the ligamentum flavum, pinching the spinal cord and producing central hemorrhagic necrosis or edema within the central pericanalicular gray and white matter. Key hallmarks include:\n1) MOTOR WEAKNESS: WEAKNESS IN THE UPPER EXTREMITIES IS FAR MORE SEVERE THAN IN THE LOWER EXTREMITIES (arms >> legs), because corticospinal fibers representing the upper limbs are located medially/centrally within the lateral corticospinal tract, closer to the central lesion, while lower limb fibers are located peripherally;\n2) SENSORY LOSS: Patchy loss of pain and temperature in the arms due to anterior white commissure disruption, with characteristic 'SACRAL SPARING' (sacral pain/temp fibers are situated at the outermost periphery of the cord).\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Anterior Cord Syndrome causes bilateral paraplegia affecting legs and trunk equally, with loss of pain/temp and sphincter control.\n- Brown-Séquard is a unilateral hemisection with asymmetric findings.\n- Posterior Cord Syndrome causes isolated dorsal column loss.\n- Cauda Equina Syndrome is a lumbosacral nerve root compression syndrome.")

add_q("inhouse_245", CAT8,
"A 25-year-old motocross racer sustains a burst fracture of the C6 vertebra with complete anatomical transection of the cervical spinal cord. In the immediate 48 hours following the trauma, he displays complete flaccid paralysis of all four limbs, complete absence of all deep tendon reflexes (areflexia), loss of anal sphincter tone, and acute urinary retention. What physiological state explains this initial flaccidity and absent reflexes following an upper motor neuron lesion, and what long-term motor changes will develop weeks later?",
["Initial state is 'Spinal Shock' (temporary cessation of all reflex activity below the lesion due to sudden loss of descending facilitation); weeks later, Spasticity, Hyperreflexia, and Extensor Plantar responses (Babinski sign) develop", "Initial state is permanent lower motor neuron death; no recovery or spasticity ever occurs", "Initial state is myasthenia gravis; treated with pyridostigmine", "Initial state is decerebrate rigidity; resolves to normal tone", "Initial state is pure sensory neuropathy; motor strength recovers completely"],
0,
"Following acute, severe spinal cord transection or injury, the nervous system enters a transient physiological state termed 'SPINAL SHOCK' lasting days to weeks (typically 1 to 4 weeks):\n1) ACUTE PHASE (SPINAL SHOCK): Characterized by complete FLACCID PARALYSIS, COMPLETE AREFLEXIA (absence of all deep tendon and cutaneous reflexes below the lesion), loss of sympathetic vascular tone (neurogenic shock: hypotension, bradycardia), and an atonic bladder with urinary retention. This occurs because spinal cord circuits below the transection are suddenly deprived of background tonic facilitation from descending supraspinal tracts (corticospinal, vestibulospinal, reticulospinal);\n2) CHRONIC PHASE (weeks to months later): As spinal interneurons and motor neurons undergo synaptic reorganization and denervation supersensitivity, reflex arcs recover and become pathologically disinhibited, transitioning into the classic chronic upper motor neuron syndrome: SPASTIC QUADRIPLEGIA, HYPERREFLEXIA, CLONUS, EXTENSOR PLANTAR RESPONSES (BABINSKI SIGN), and AUTONOMIC DYSREFLEXIA (in lesions above T6).\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- The flaccidity of spinal shock is temporary; chronic spasticity always develops.\n- Transection does not cause myasthenia gravis.\n- Decerebrate rigidity occurs with midbrain lesions, not acute flaccid spinal shock.\n- Motor strength does not recover after complete anatomical transection.")


# Q246 - Q260
add_q("inhouse_246", CAT8,
"A 42-year-old construction worker is brought to the emergency department after a heavy steel beam falls across his lower back. MRI of the lumbar spine confirms a massive central disc herniation at the L4-L5 level compressing the lumbosacral nerve roots. Neurological examination reveals severe bilateral radicular pain shooting down both legs, asymmetric flaccid weakness of ankle dorsiflexion and plantarflexion, absent patellar and Achilles deep tendon reflexes bilaterally, asymmetric patchy sensory loss in the perianal region and thighs, and urinary retention with overflow incontinence. What is the diagnosis, and why is emergent neurosurgical decompression required?",
["Cauda Equina Syndrome; compression of lumbosacral nerve roots below the conus medullaris requires emergent decompression within 24-48 hours to prevent permanent bladder/bowel denervation and paraplegia", "Conus Medullaris syndrome; injury to the sacral spinal cord segments with pure upper motor neuron signs", "L5 radiculopathy alone; managed conservatively with physical therapy", "Guillain-Barré syndrome; managed with intravenous immunoglobulin", "Brown-Séquard syndrome; managed with high-dose corticosteroids alone"],
0,
"CAUDA EQUINA SYNDROME is a true neurosurgical emergency caused by compression of the multiple lumbosacral nerve roots (the cauda equina) within the spinal canal below the termination of the spinal cord (conus medullaris terminates at L1-L2 vertebra). Hallmarks include:\n1) Severe, often asymmetric RADICULAR LEG PAIN;\n2) Lower Motor Neuron (LMN) FLACCID WEAKNESS of the lower extremities with AREFLEXIA (absent patellar and Achilles reflexes);\n3) SADDLE ANESTHESIA (sensory loss over dermatomes S3-S5: perianal region, perineum, buttocks, inner thighs);\n4) AUTONOMIC SPHINCTER DYSFUNCTION (urinary retention with overflow incontinence and loss of anal sphincter tone). Emergent surgical decompression (laminectomy/discectomy) within 24 to 48 hours is mandatory to prevent permanent neurogenic bladder, fecal incontinence, and sexual dysfunction.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Conus Medullaris syndrome occurs at L1-L2 vertebra, presents with sudden, symmetric saddle anesthesia, early bowel/bladder dysfunction, but displays mixed UMN/LMN or prominent hyperreflexia/Babinski, whereas Cauda Equina is purely LMN areflexic.\n- Isolated L5 radiculopathy does not cause saddle anesthesia or urinary retention.\n- Guillain-Barré is an ascending autoimmune polyneuropathy without acute traumatic lumbar compression.\n- Brown-Séquard is cord hemisection.")

add_q("inhouse_247", CAT8,
"A 48-year-old accountant complains of severe, electric-shock-like pain shooting from his neck down his right arm into his thumb and index finger. Neurological examination reveals sensory loss in the C6 dermatome, weakness in the biceps and brachioradialis muscles, and a diminished biceps reflex. A radiologist confirms a right posterolateral disc herniation between the C5 and C6 cervical vertebrae. What is the fundamental pathological definition of a 'radiculopathy'?",
["Dysfunction, mechanical compression, or chemical inflammation of a single SPINAL NERVE ROOT as it exits the neural foramen, producing pain and sensory deficits in a dermatomal distribution and weakness in a myotomal distribution", "Autoimmune destruction of the neuromuscular junction", "Infarction of the primary somatosensory cortex", "Compression of a peripheral nerve trunk in an anatomical fibro-osseous tunnel", "Infection of the dorsal column nuclei by spirochetes"],
0,
"A RADICULOPATHY is defined as pathology (mechanical nerve root compression by a herniated intervertebral disc or osteophytic bone spur, or chemical inflammation) affecting a SPINAL NERVE ROOT as it emerges from the dural sac and traverses the intervertebral neural foramen. It is clinically recognized by a characteristic triad:\n1) RADICULAR PAIN AND SENSORY DEFICITS restricted to the corresponding DERMATOME (the specific skin region supplied by that single spinal nerve root);\n2) MOTOR WEAKNESS restricted to the corresponding MYOTOME (the group of skeletal muscles innervated by that root);\n3) HYPOREFLEXIA or absence of the deep tendon reflex mediated by that specific root.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- NMJ destruction describes Myasthenia Gravis.\n- Cortical infarction causes hemisensory deficits across multiple dermatomes, not a single root pattern.\n- Compression in a fibro-osseous tunnel describes an ENTRAPMENT NEUROPATHY (e.g., median nerve in carpal tunnel), which follows a peripheral nerve distribution, not a dermatome.\n- Dorsal column nuclei infection describes tabes dorsalis.")

add_q("inhouse_248", CAT8,
"A 50-year-old carpenter presents with shooting neck pain that radiates across his shoulder, down the lateral aspect of his right forearm, and into his THUMB and INDEX FINGER. On physical examination, muscle testing reveals weakness (4/5) in elbow flexion (biceps brachii and brachioradialis) and wrist extension. The right Biceps reflex and Brachioradialis reflex are diminished (1+ compared to 2+ on the left). A posterolateral cervical disc herniation is suspected. Which spinal nerve root is compressed, and between which two vertebrae has the disc herniated?",
["C6 spinal nerve root; herniated disc between C5 and C6 vertebrae", "C5 spinal nerve root; herniated disc between C4 and C5 vertebrae", "C7 spinal nerve root; herniated disc between C6 and C7 vertebrae", "C8 spinal nerve root; herniated disc between C7 and T1 vertebrae", "T1 spinal nerve root; herniated disc between T1 and T2 vertebrae"],
0,
"C6 RADICULOPATHY is one of the most common cervical radiculopathies. In the cervical spine, nerve roots exit ABOVE their corresponding vertebrae; therefore, a posterolateral disc herniation at the C5-C6 INTERSPACE compresses the exiting C6 NERVE ROOT. The classic clinical presentation of C6 radiculopathy includes:\n- Sensory deficit: Numbness, paresthesias, and radicular pain radiating down the lateral arm and forearm into the THUMB and INDEX FINGER (C6 dermatome, mnemonic: C6 forms the 'six-shooter' thumb/index finger);\n- Motor deficit: Weakness of elbow flexors (biceps brachii, brachioradialis) and wrist extensors (extensor carpi radialis longus/brevis);\n- Reflex deficit: Diminished or absent BICEPS REFLEX and BRACHIORADIALIS REFLEX.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- C5 radiculopathy (C4-C5 disc) affects shoulder/deltoid (abduction) and lateral upper arm, with biceps reflex depression, sparing the thumb.\n- C7 radiculopathy (C6-C7 disc) affects the middle finger and triceps (elbow extension).\n- C8 radiculopathy (C7-T1 disc) affects the little finger and intrinsic hand muscles.\n- T1 radiculopathy affects medial arm.")

add_q("inhouse_249", CAT8,
"A 54-year-old tennis player complains of sharp pain radiating from his neck down the back of his arm and forearm into his MIDDLE FINGER. Physical examination reveals weakness of elbow extension (triceps brachii 3+/5) and wrist flexion. The right Triceps deep tendon reflex is completely absent (0/4), while biceps and brachioradialis reflexes are normal. Sensation to pinprick is diminished exclusively on the palmar and dorsal aspects of the middle finger. Which cervical nerve root is compressed?",
["C7 spinal nerve root", "C5 spinal nerve root", "C6 spinal nerve root", "C8 spinal nerve root", "T1 spinal nerve root"],
0,
"C7 RADICULOPATHY is the SINGLE MOST COMMON cervical radiculopathy (accounting for approximately 60% of all cases), caused by a posterolateral disc herniation at the C6-C7 intervertebral space compressing the exiting C7 NERVE ROOT. Key diagnostic hallmarks include:\n- Sensory deficit: Pain, paresthesias, and sensory loss centered on the MIDDLE FINGER (C7 dermatome);\n- Motor deficit: Weakness of elbow extension (TRICEPS BRACHII) and wrist flexors (flexor carpi radialis);\n- Reflex deficit: Diminished or absent TRICEPS REFLEX (mediated by C7).\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- C5 radiculopathy affects deltoid and lateral arm, not middle finger.\n- C6 radiculopathy affects thumb/index finger and biceps reflex.\n- C8 radiculopathy affects little/ring fingers and intrinsic hand muscles.\n- T1 radiculopathy affects medial arm.")

add_q("inhouse_250", CAT8,
"A 46-year-old woman presents with numbness and tingling along the medial (ulnar) border of her left forearm, extending into her RING and LITTLE FINGERS. Physical examination reveals intact biceps and triceps strength and normal deep tendon reflexes. However, she has prominent weakness in finger abduction and adduction (dorsal and palmar interossei muscles) and weak thumb opposition. An MRI confirms a posterolateral disc herniation between the C7 and T1 vertebrae. Which nerve root is involved?",
["C8 spinal nerve root", "C6 spinal nerve root", "C7 spinal nerve root", "T2 spinal nerve root", "Radial nerve alone"],
0,
"C8 RADICULOPATHY results from compression of the C8 nerve root as it exits the neural foramen between the C7 and T1 vertebrae. The clinical features of C8 radiculopathy include:\n- Sensory deficit: Pain, numbness, and paresthesias radiating down the medial (ulnar) aspect of the forearm and hand into the RING AND LITTLE FINGERS (digits 4 and 5, C8 dermatome);\n- Motor deficit: Weakness of the INTRINSIC HAND MUSCLES (finger abductors and adductors: dorsal and palmar interossei, thenar and hypothenar muscles) and finger flexors (flexor digitorum profundus/superficialis);\n- Reflex deficit: There is no isolated, reliable deep tendon reflex for C8 (finger flexion reflex is sometimes tested, but triceps [C7] and biceps [C5/C6] reflexes are completely normal).\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- C6 affects thumb/index finger.\n- C7 affects middle finger and triceps reflex.\n- T2 affects the axilla and upper chest.\n- Radial nerve lesion causes wrist drop (extensors), sparing finger abduction/interossei.")

add_q("inhouse_251", CAT8,
"A 56-year-old male presents with sharp, shooting pain radiating from his lower back across the anterior thigh down to his medial knee and medial shin. Physical examination reveals weakness in knee extension (quadriceps femoris 3/5). The right patellar (knee-jerk) reflex is absent (0/4), whereas the Achilles reflex is brisk and normal. Sensation to pinprick is diminished over the anterior thigh, patella, and medial malleolus. An MRI confirms a posterolateral disc herniation at L3-L4 compressing which spinal nerve root?",
["L4 spinal nerve root", "L2 spinal nerve root", "L3 spinal nerve root", "L5 spinal nerve root", "S1 spinal nerve root"],
0,
"L4 RADICULOPATHY is typically caused by a posterolateral disc herniation at the L3-L4 interspace compressing the L4 nerve root. Diagnostic hallmarks of L4 radiculopathy include:\n- Sensory deficit: Pain and paresthesias radiating down the anterior thigh, across the anterior knee (patella), and down the medial leg to the MEDIAL MALLEOLUS (L4 dermatome, mnemonic: L4 hits the floor on the medial side);\n- Motor deficit: Weakness of knee extension (QUADRICEPS FEMORIS) and ankle dorsiflexion (tibialis anterior);\n- Reflex deficit: Diminished or absent PATELLAR TENDON REFLEX (knee-jerk, mediated by L3-L4 via the femoral nerve).\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- L2/L3 roots supply upper anterior thigh; patellar reflex is partially preserved.\n- L5 radiculopathy causes foot drop and lateral leg numbness, with a NORMAL patellar reflex.\n- S1 radiculopathy causes gastrocnemius weakness and absent Achilles reflex.")

add_q("inhouse_252", CAT8,
"A 45-year-old man presents with severe lower back pain radiating down the lateral aspect of his right leg across the DORSUM OF HIS FOOT to his BIG TOE. On physical examination, he has difficulty walking on his heels due to weakness in ankle dorsiflexion (FOOT DROP, tibialis anterior 3/5) and cannot extend his great toe against resistance (extensor hallucis longus 2/5). Both his patellar reflex and Achilles reflex are brisk and completely normal (2+ bilaterally). MRI confirms a paracentral disc herniation at the L4-L5 level. Which nerve root is compressed?",
["L5 spinal nerve root", "L3 spinal nerve root", "L4 spinal nerve root", "S1 spinal nerve root", "S2 spinal nerve root"],
0,
"L5 RADICULOPATHY is the SINGLE MOST COMMON lumbar radiculopathy, typically caused by a paracentral disc herniation at the L4-L5 intervertebral space compressing the transiting L5 nerve root. Hallmarks of L5 radiculopathy include:\n- Sensory deficit: Pain, numbness, and paresthesias radiating down the lateral thigh, anterolateral leg, across the DORSUM OF THE FOOT to the GREAT TOE (L5 dermatome);\n- Motor deficit: WEAKNESS OF ANKLE DORSIFLEXION (FOOT DROP, tibialis anterior), WEAKNESS OF GREAT TOE EXTENSION (EXTENSOR HALLUCIS LONGUS, EHL), and weakness of foot eversion/inversion and hip abduction;\n- Reflex deficit: DEEP TENDON REFLEXES ARE NORMAL: there is no reliable, standard deep tendon reflex mediated primarily by L5 (the patellar reflex is L4; the Achilles reflex is S1).\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- L3 affects anterior mid-thigh.\n- L4 affects anterior knee/medial malleolus and depresses the patellar reflex.\n- S1 affects the sole of the foot and depresses the Achilles reflex.\n- S2 affects the posterior thigh and popliteal fossa.")

add_q("inhouse_253", CAT8,
"A 52-year-old marathon runner presents with sharp, shooting pain radiating down the posterior aspect of his right buttock, posterior thigh, and calf, extending into the lateral border of his foot and the SOLE OF HIS FOOT. On physical examination, he cannot walk on his toes due to weakness in ankle plantarflexion (gastrocnemius/soleus 3/5). The right Achilles (ankle-jerk) reflex is completely absent (0/4), while the patellar reflex is normal (2+). Sensation to pinprick is diminished over the lateral foot and sole. An MRI confirms a posterolateral disc herniation at the L5-S1 interspace. Which nerve root is compressed?",
["S1 spinal nerve root", "L4 spinal nerve root", "L5 spinal nerve root", "S3 spinal nerve root", "S4 spinal nerve root"],
0,
"S1 RADICULOPATHY is the second most common lumbar radiculopathy, caused by a posterolateral disc herniation at the L5-S1 interspace compressing the transiting S1 nerve root. Diagnostic hallmarks include:\n- Sensory deficit: Radicular pain and numbness radiating down the posterior thigh, posterior calf, into the LATERAL MALLEOLUS, LATERAL FOOT, and SOLE OF THE FOOT / LITTLE TOE (S1 dermatome);\n- Motor deficit: Weakness of ANKLE PLANTARFLEXION (GASTROCNEMIUS AND SOLEUS muscles; patient CANNOT WALK ON TOES);\n- Reflex deficit: Diminished or ABSENT ACHILLES TENDON REFLEX (ankle-jerk reflex, mediated by S1 via the tibial nerve).\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- L4 affects the anterior knee and depresses the patellar reflex.\n- L5 affects the dorsum of the foot and big toe; patient cannot walk on heels (foot drop), but Achilles reflex is normal.\n- S3/S4 roots mediate saddle/perianal sensation and bowel/bladder sphincters.")

add_q("inhouse_254", CAT8,
"A 32-year-old soccer player strikes his lateral knee against a goalpost and presents with a right foot drop (inability to dorsiflex the ankle) and numbness over the dorsum of his foot. The neurologist must distinguish whether this patient has a COMMON PERONEAL (FIBULAR) NERVE LESION at the fibular neck or an L5 RADICULOPATHY. Both conditions produce foot drop and weak foot eversion. Which manual muscle testing finding definitively confirms an L5 RADICULOPATHY rather than a common peroneal nerve injury?",
["Weakness of FOOT INVERSION (tibialis posterior) and weak HIP ABDUCTION (gluteus medius)", "Weakness of ankle dorsiflexion (tibialis anterior)", "Weakness of great toe extension (extensor hallucis longus)", "Weakness of foot eversion (peroneus longus)", "Intact knee extension (quadriceps)"],
0,
"This is one of the highest-yield clinical distinctions in clinical neurology and board examinations:\n- Both common peroneal nerve compression (at the fibular head) and L5 radiculopathy cause FOOT DROP (weakness of ankle dorsiflexion via tibialis anterior) and impaired foot eversion (peroneus longus/brevis);\n- IN A COMMON PERONEAL NERVE LESION: Foot INVERSION is completely NORMAL, because the tibialis posterior muscle is innervated by the TIBIAL NERVE (not peroneal); Hip ABDUCTION is completely NORMAL, because the gluteus medius is innervated by the SUPERIOR GLUTEAL NERVE;\n- IN AN L5 RADICULOPATHY: Because the L5 nerve root supplies the common peroneal, tibial, and superior gluteal nerves, L5 radiculopathy causes WEAKNESS OF FOOT INVERSION (tibialis posterior, L5) AND WEAKNESS OF HIP ABDUCTION (gluteus medius, L5 via superior gluteal nerve). Trendelenburg sign may be positive.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Ankle dorsiflexion, EHL, and eversion are weak in both conditions.\n- Knee extension is intact in both (L4 femoral nerve).")

add_q("inhouse_255", CAT8,
"A 44-year-old warehouse worker with acute lower back pain undergoes physical examination. With the patient lying supine, the physician passively elevates the patient's symptomatic right leg with the knee fully extended. At 45 degrees of hip flexion, the maneuver reproduces the patient's sharp, lancinating pain shooting down the posterior leg past the knee into the foot (positive Straight Leg Raise test / Lasègue sign). When the physician performs the same maneuver on the asymptomatic LEFT leg, the patient experiences sharp pain shooting down the symptomatic RIGHT leg (positive Crossed Straight Leg Raise test). What anatomical structure is stressed by these maneuvers, and what does the positive Crossed Straight Leg Raise test indicate?",
["The maneuver stretches the L5 and S1 spinal nerve roots over a herniated lumbar disc; the Crossed Straight Leg Raise test has high specificity (>90%) for disc herniation", "The maneuver stretches the femoral nerve over the inguinal ligament", "The maneuver contracts the iliopsoas muscle, causing psoas abscess pain", "The maneuver stresses the sacroiliac joint exclusively", "The maneuver compresses the sciatic nerve in the piriformis canal without disc involvement"],
0,
"The STRAIGHT LEG RAISE (SLR) TEST (Lasègue sign) is a mechanical neurodynamic test for lumbosacral radiculopathy (L5 or S1 nerve root irritation). When the extended leg is passively raised between 30° and 70°, the sciatic nerve and its contributing nerve roots (L5, S1) are pulled taut and stretched against a herniated intervertebral disc. Reproduction of radicular pain radiating below the knee confirms nerve root tension (high sensitivity ~90%, but moderate specificity). The CROSSED STRAIGHT LEG RAISE (Well Leg Raise) test occurs when passively elevating the asymptomatic, non-painful leg reproduces shooting radicular pain in the contralateral symptomatic leg (due to pulling of the dural sac across the midline). The crossed SLR has extremely HIGH SPECIFICITY (>90%) for a herniated disc.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Femoral nerve is stretched by the reverse straight leg raise (prone knee bend, for L2-L4 roots).\n- Iliopsoas test evaluates appendicitis/psoas abscess.\n- Patrick/FABER test stresses the sacroiliac joint.\n- Piriformis syndrome causes buttocks pain, but does not yield a positive crossed SLR.")

add_q("inhouse_256", CAT8,
"A spine surgeon reviews axial MRI scans of two different patients with lumbar disc herniations at the L4-L5 level. Patient A has a typical PARACENTRAL (posterolateral) disc herniation, whereas Patient B has a FAR LATERAL (foraminal) disc herniation. In the lumbar spine, which nerve root is compressed in Patient A, and which nerve root is compressed in Patient B?",
["Patient A (paracentral) compresses the PASSING / TRANSITING nerve root (L5 root); Patient B (far lateral) compresses the EXITING nerve root (L4 root)", "Patient A compresses L4; Patient B compresses L5", "Both patients compress the L4 root exclusively", "Both patients compress the L5 root exclusively", "Patient A compresses S1; Patient B compresses L3"],
0,
"In the lumbar spine, nerve roots exit the neural foramen BELOW their correspondingly numbered vertebrae (e.g., L4 root exits beneath the L4 pedicle, above the L4-L5 disc):\n1) PARACENTRAL (POSTEROLATERAL) DISC HERNIATION (the standard 90% location): The herniated disc protrudes into the lateral recess of the spinal canal, MISSING the already-exited L4 root and COMPRESSING THE TRANSITING / PASSING NERVE ROOT that is coursing down toward the next foramen: THE L5 NERVE ROOT. Thus, an L4-L5 paracentral disc causes L5 radiculopathy;\n2) FAR LATERAL (EXTRAFORAMINAL) DISC HERNIATION: The disc herniates directly into or lateral to the neural foramen, directly COMPRESSING THE EXITING NERVE ROOT: THE L4 NERVE ROOT. Thus, an L4-L5 far lateral disc causes L4 radiculopathy.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Paracentral compresses the lower transiting root (L5), not the exiting root (L4).\n- The roots compressed are different due to spinal canal geometry.\n- S1 transits past the L5-S1 disc, not L4-L5.")

add_q("inhouse_257", CAT8,
"A 58-year-old man experiencing an acute inferior myocardial infarction reports severe, crushing substernal chest pressure with deep, aching pain radiating down the medial aspect of his left arm and into his left jaw. What neuroanatomical mechanism explains why myocardial ischemia produces referred pain felt in the left arm and chest?",
["Visceral sensory afferents from the ischemic heart enter the upper thoracic spinal cord (T1-T4) and converge onto the same second-order spinothalamic dorsal horn neurons that receive somatic cutaneous pain from the left chest and arm (dermatomal convergence theory)", "Cardiac pain fibers travel through the vagus nerve and stimulate the brachial plexus directly", "Ischemic myocardial toxins leak into the pericardial fluid and irritate the phrenic nerve", "The heart and the left arm are supplied by the same peripheral motor nerve", "Coronary artery vasospasm causes reflex spasm of the left radial artery"],
0,
"REFERRED VISCERAL PAIN is explained by the CONVERGENCE-PROJECTION THEORY of pain:\n1) Visceral pain afferents from the ischemic myocardium travel with sympathetic nerves (cardiac sympathetic nerves) to enter the spinal cord at the T1 through T4/T5 thoracic segments;\n2) In the dorsal horn, these visceral primary afferents SYNAPSE ONTO THE SAME SECOND-ORDER SPINOTHALAMIC NEURONS that receive somatic nociceptive inputs from the cutaneous T1-T4 dermatomes (which cover the anterior chest wall and the medial aspect of the left arm and forearm);\n3) Because the cerebral cortex receives frequent somatic tactile/pain signals from the skin but rarely from internal viscera, the brain projects the conscious percept of pain to the SOMATIC DERMATOME corresponding to that spinal segment (left chest and medial arm).\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Vagus nerve carries parasympathetic afferents mediating nausea/sweating, but pain travels with sympathetic fibers (T1-T4).\n- Phrenic nerve (C3-C5) refers diaphragmatic pain to the shoulder, not cardiac T1-T4 arm pain.\n- The heart is not innervated by peripheral somatic motor nerves.\n- Radial artery spasm does not cause referred angina.")

add_q("inhouse_258", CAT8,
"A clinical neurologist maps sensory dermatomal levels on an unconscious trauma patient. Which of the following correctly pairs key anatomical landmarks with their defining spinal dermatome levels?",
["Nipple line = T4; Umbilicus = T10; Inguinal ligament = L1; Great toe = L5", "Nipple line = T10; Umbilicus = T4; Inguinal ligament = S1; Great toe = L4", "Nipple line = T2; Umbilicus = T8; Inguinal ligament = L3; Great toe = S1", "Nipple line = C6; Umbilicus = C8; Inguinal ligament = T10; Great toe = L1", "Nipple line = T6; Umbilicus = T12; Inguinal ligament = L2; Great toe = S2"],
0,
"High-yield dermatomal landmarks are critical for localizing spinal cord lesions:\n- C2: Occiput and posterior skull\n- C6: Thumb and index finger ('six-shooter')\n- C7: Middle finger\n- C8: Little finger and medial hand\n- T4: NIPPLE LINE (mnemonic: 'T4 at the Teat')\n- T10: UMBILICUS (mnemonic: 'T10 at the Belly But-ten')\n- L1: INGUINAL LIGAMENT / GROIN (mnemonic: 'L1 for Ligament')\n- L4: Anterior knee and medial malleolus\n- L5: Lateral leg, dorsum of foot, and GREAT TOE\n- S1: Lateral foot, sole of foot, and little toe\n- S2-S4: Perianal / saddle region (pudendal nerve).\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- T4 is nipples, T10 is umbilicus (not reversed).\n- T2 is upper chest/axilla.\n- C6 is thumb, C8 is little finger.\n- T6 is xiphoid process.")

add_q("inhouse_259", CAT8,
"During an electromyography (EMG) evaluation of a patient with suspected motor neuron disease, the neurophysiologist distinguishes between 'fasciculations' and 'fibrillations.' What is the clinical and physiological difference between these two spontaneous motor activities?",
["Fasciculations are spontaneous contractions of an entire motor unit that are VISIBLE to the naked eye under the skin; Fibrillations are spontaneous contractions of single denervated muscle fibers that are INVISIBLE to the naked eye and detected ONLY by needle EMG", "Fasciculations are invisible; Fibrillations are visible to the naked eye", "Fasciculations indicate upper motor neuron disease; Fibrillations indicate myasthenia gravis", "Fasciculations indicate normal muscle relaxation; Fibrillations indicate hyperthyroidism", "There is no electrophysiological difference; the terms are synonymous"],
0,
"This is a fundamental clinical distinction in neurology and electrodiagnosis:\n- FASCICULATIONS: Spontaneous, synchronous contractions of an ENTIRE MOTOR UNIT (all muscle fibers innervated by a single motor neuron). They are macroscopic and VISIBLE TO THE NAKED EYE as spontaneous ripples, twitches, or jumps under the skin. They indicate spontaneous depolarization of a diseased or irritable lower motor neuron soma or proximal axon (seen in ALS, radiculopathies, benign fasciculation syndrome);\n- FIBRILLATIONS: Spontaneous, rhythmic contractions of an INDIVIDUAL, SINGLE MUSCLE FIBER that has lost its motor innervation (denervation). Because a single muscle fiber generates microscopic tension, fibrillations are COMPLETELY INVISIBLE TO THE NAKED EYE. They can be detected ONLY by inserting a needle electrode into the muscle (needle EMG), where they appear as regular, biphasic/triphasic fibrillation potentials or positive sharp waves, serving as the gold standard electrophysiological sign of ACTIVE, ONGOING AXONAL DENERVATION.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Fasciculations are visible; fibrillations are invisible.\n- Both indicate lower motor neuron/axonal pathology, not UMN or MG.\n- Fasciculations represent abnormal spontaneous firing, not normal relaxation.\n- The terms are distinct clinical and electrophysiological entities.")

add_q("inhouse_260", CAT8,
"A 42-year-old woman undergoes nerve conduction studies (NCS) for severe symmetric numbness and weakness in all four limbs. The neurophysiologist explains the electrophysiological differences between a primary 'demyelinating' neuropathy and a primary 'axonal' neuropathy. Which set of NCS findings indicates a primary DEMYELINATING polyneuropathy (such as Guillain-Barré syndrome / AIDP)?",
["Markedly slowed conduction velocities (<35-40 m/s), prolonged distal latencies, temporal dispersion, and conduction block, with relatively preserved baseline CMAP amplitudes", "Normal conduction velocities with severely reduced CMAP and SNAP amplitudes", "Absence of all sensory potentials with normal motor velocities", "Fasciculations with normal conduction velocities", "Continuous high-frequency discharges without latency prolongation"],
0,
"Nerve Conduction Studies (NCS) clearly differentiate demyelinating from axonal neuropathies:\n1) PRIMARY DEMYELINATING NEUROPATHY (e.g., Guillain-Barré / AIDP, CIDP, Charcot-Marie-Tooth type 1): The pathology targets the myelin sheath (Schwann cells). Because myelin determines conduction velocity and time constant, loss of myelin causes: MARKED SLOWING OF CONDUCTION VELOCITIES (often <35-40 m/s), PROLONGATION OF DISTAL MOTOR LATENCIES, TEMPORAL DISPERSION (broadening of the waveform due to unequal conduction across fibers), and CONDUCTION BLOCK. In pure demyelination without axonal loss, total CMAP amplitude upon distal stimulation is relatively PRESERVED;\n2) PRIMARY AXONAL NEUROPATHY (e.g., diabetic polyneuropathy, toxic, alcoholic): The pathology targets the axon itself. As axons die back, the surviving axons conduct at near-normal speeds (>40-45 m/s); however, because fewer total muscle fibers are recruited, the COMPOUND MUSCLE ACTION POTENTIAL (CMAP) AND SENSORY ACTION POTENTIAL (SNAP) AMPLITUDES ARE MARKEDLY REDUCED.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Normal velocities with reduced CMAP amplitudes describes a primary AXONAL neuropathy.\n- Absence of SNAPs with normal motor describes a pure sensory neuronopathy.\n- Fasciculations are an EMG finding of motor neuron disease.\n- Continuous discharges describe neuromyotonia.")


# Q261 - Q275
add_q("inhouse_261", CAT9,
"A neuroanatomy professor reviews the skull base foramina through which the three major divisions of the Trigeminal Nerve (CN V) exit the cranial cavity. Which sequence correctly matches V1 (ophthalmic), V2 (maxillary), and V3 (mandibular) with their respective skull exit foramina?",
["V1: Superior Orbital Fissure; V2: Foramen Rotundum; V3: Foramen Ovale (mnemonic: Standing Room Only - SRO)", "V1: Foramen Ovale; V2: Foramen Spinosum; V3: Foramen Magnum", "V1: Optic Canal; V2: Foramen Lacerum; V3: Jugular Foramen", "V1: Foramen Rotundum; V2: Superior Orbital Fissure; V3: Internal Acoustic Meatus", "V1: Carotid Canal; V2: Foramen Ovale; V3: Hypoglossal Canal"],
0,
"The three divisions of the trigeminal nerve exit the skull base through three distinct apertures in the sphenoid bone, recalled by the classic mnemonic 'Standing Room Only' (SRO):\n1) V1 (Ophthalmic division): Exits through the SUPERIOR ORBITAL FISSURE into the orbit;\n2) V2 (Maxillary division): Exits through the FORAMEN ROTUNDUM into the pterygopalatine fossa;\n3) V3 (Mandibular division): Exits through the FORAMEN OVALE into the infratemporal fossa.\nCrucially, V1 and V2 are purely sensory, whereas V3 is a MIXED NERVE carrying both sensory fibers from the lower face and motor efferents to the muscles of mastication.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Foramen spinosum transmits the middle meningeal artery.\n- Optic canal transmits CN II and ophthalmic artery; jugular foramen transmits CN IX, X, XI.\n- Foramen rotundum transmits V2, not V1; internal acoustic meatus transmits CN VII and VIII.\n- Carotid canal transmits ICA; hypoglossal canal transmits CN XII.")

add_q("inhouse_262", CAT9,
"A 42-year-old woman undergoing evaluation for facial numbness has pinprick testing performed across her face and neck. Sensation is completely absent over her forehead, cheek, and chin. However, sensation to pinprick is completely INTACT over the skin overlying the angle of her mandible. Why is sensation over the angle of the mandible spared in a complete trigeminal nerve lesion?",
["The angle of the mandible is innervated by the Greater Auricular Nerve (C2-C3 of the cervical plexus), NOT by the trigeminal nerve", "The angle of the mandible is innervated by the facial nerve (CN VII)", "The angle of the mandible is innervated by the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)", "The angle of the mandible is innervated by sympathetic fibers from the superior cervical ganglion", "The angle of the mandible is innervated by the vagus nerve (CN X)"],
0,
"This is a classic clinical pearl and board trap: the cutaneous sensory innervation of the face is supplied almost entirely by the three divisions of Cranial Nerve V (V1, V2, V3). However, the skin overlying the ANGLE OF THE MANDIBLE (and the lower portion of the earlobe) is an exception: it is innervated by the GREATER AURICULAR NERVE and LESSER OCCIPITAL NERVE, which arise from the CERVICAL SPINAL PLEXUS (C2-C3 roots). Therefore, in a complete trigeminal nerve lesion, sensation over the angle of the mandible remains completely INTACT, allowing clinicians to verify that the deficit is truly cranial and not a non-organic pattern.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- CN VII provides motor innervation to muscles of facial expression and taste to anterior 2/3 of tongue, not somatic sensation to the angle of the mandible.\n- CN IX innervates the posterior 1/3 of the tongue, tonsils, and pharynx.\n- Sympathetic fibers mediate sweating and vasodilation, not somatic pinprick sensation.\n- CN X innervates the external acoustic meatus and larynx/pharynx.")

add_q("inhouse_263", CAT9,
"The brainstem contains four distinct nuclei associated with the Trigeminal Nerve (CN V). Which nucleus contains primary pseudounipolar sensory cell bodies located entirely WITHIN the central nervous system, and which nucleus processes pain and temperature from the face?",
["Mesencephalic nucleus contains primary cell bodies inside the CNS; Spinal trigeminal nucleus processes facial pain and temperature", "Chief sensory nucleus contains primary cell bodies; Motor nucleus processes pain", "Spinal trigeminal nucleus contains primary cell bodies; Mesencephalic nucleus processes pain", "Motor nucleus contains primary cell bodies; Chief sensory nucleus processes pain", "Solitary nucleus contains primary cell bodies; Vestibular nucleus processes pain"],
0,
"The trigeminal nuclear complex has unique anatomical features:\n1) MESENCEPHALIC NUCLEUS OF V: Located in the midbrain. It is COMPLETELY UNIQUE IN HUMAN ANATOMY because it consists of primary pseudounipolar sensory cell bodies located INSIDE THE CNS (representing neural crest cells that failed to migrate into the periphery). It receives proprioceptive afferents from muscles of mastication, periodontal ligaments, and extraocular muscles, mediating the jaw-jerk reflex;\n2) SPINAL TRIGEMINAL NUCLEUS: Extends from the pons through the medulla down to C2-C3 in the cervical spinal cord. Its caudal portion (pars caudalis) processes PAIN AND TEMPERATURE from the face, ipsilateral oral cavity, and meninges;\n3) PRINCIPAL (CHIEF) SENSORY NUCLEUS: Located in the mid-pons; processes fine discriminative touch and vibration;\n4) MOTOR NUCLEUS OF V: Located in the mid-pons; innervates muscles of mastication.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Primary cell bodies for touch and pain reside in the peripheral Trigeminal Ganglion outside the CNS.\n- Motor nucleus contains lower motor neurons, not sensory pain neurons.\n- Solitary nucleus processes taste (CN VII, IX, X) and visceral reflexes.")

add_q("inhouse_264", CAT9,
"A 35-year-old woman involved in a diving accident sustains a focal traumatic injury to the upper cervical spinal cord at the C2-C3 level. Sensory examination reveals complete loss of pain and temperature sensation affecting the outer perimeter of her face (ears, hairline, lateral cheeks, and jaw), while pain and temperature sensation around her nose, lips, and mouth is completely PRESERVED ('onion-skin' sensory loss). What anatomical organization of the spinal trigeminal nucleus explains this pattern?",
["The spinal trigeminal nucleus pars caudalis is organized somatotopically in concentric layers: perioral areas are represented rostrally in the medulla, while outer facial borders are represented caudally in the upper cervical cord (C2-C3)", "V1 fibers terminate in the cervical cord, while V3 fibers terminate in the pons", "Pain fibers from the lips decussate across the corpus callosum", "Facial skin receptors are distributed in concentric dermatomes derived from cervical roots", "The trigeminal ganglion is arranged in concentric layers around the internal carotid artery"],
0,
"The spinal trigeminal nucleus pars caudalis exhibits a unique somatotopic organization known as the 'ONION-SKIN' (BALACLAVA) PATTERN:\n- The central facial areas (perioral region, lips, and nose) are represented in the ROSTRAL part of the pars caudalis (in the caudal medulla);\n- As one moves outward toward the periphery of the face (cheeks, forehead, chin, ears, and hairline), the representation moves progressively CAUDAL into the lower medulla and UPPER CERVICAL SPINAL CORD (C2-C3).\nConsequently, a lesion in the upper cervical cord (C2-C3) damages the most caudal portion of the spinal trigeminal nucleus, selectively destroying pain and temperature sensation from the OUTER PERIMETER OF THE FACE, while completely sparing perioral sensation (onion-skin sensory dissociation).\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Trigeminal division branches (V1, V2, V3) all project throughout the length of the nucleus; the rostrocaudal segregation is concentric/conical, not divisional.\n- Pain fibers cross in the brainstem, not corpus callosum.\n- Face dermatomes are V1, V2, V3; onion-skin is central nuclear somatotopy.\n- Trigeminal ganglion lies in Meckel cave, not around ICA.")

add_q("inhouse_265", CAT9,
"During a neurological examination of a comatose patient, a physician tests the corneal reflex by touching a fine wisp of sterile cotton to the lateral cornea of the right eye. The patient blinks his left eye, but the right eye DOES NOT BLINK. When the cotton wisp is touched to the left cornea, the patient blinks his left eye, but the right eye AGAIN DOES NOT BLINK. What is the anatomical localization of the lesion?",
["Right Facial Nerve (CN VII) motor efferent defect", "Right Trigeminal Nerve (CN V1) sensory afferent defect", "Left Trigeminal Nerve (CN V1) sensory afferent defect", "Left Facial Nerve (CN VII) motor efferent defect", "Bilateral Edinger-Westphal nuclei lesion"],
0,
"The CORNEAL REFLEX pathway consists of:\n1) AFFERENT LIMB: Cranial Nerve V1 (Ophthalmic division, nasociliary branch) senses the corneal touch and projects to the spinal trigeminal nucleus;\n2) CENTRAL INTERNEURONS: Interneurons in the brainstem project BILATERALLY to the facial motor nuclei (CN VII);\n3) EFFERENT LIMB: Cranial Nerve VII (Facial nerve) carries somatic motor efferents to the BILATERAL ORBICULARIS OCULI muscles, causing BOTH eyes to blink simultaneously.\nIn this patient:\n- Touching the right cornea elicited a blink in the left eye, proving that the RIGHT AFFERENT LIMB (CN V1) is completely INTACT;\n- Touching the left cornea also failed to blink the right eye, proving that the failure of the right eye to blink is an EFFERENT DEFECT: a lesion of the RIGHT FACIAL NERVE (CN VII) preventing right orbicularis oculi contraction.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Right CN V1 lesion would abolish the blink in BOTH eyes when touching the right cornea.\n- Left CN V1 lesion would abolish both blinks when touching the left cornea.\n- Left CN VII lesion would prevent the left eye from blinking.\n- Edinger-Westphal nucleus mediates pupillary light constriction, not corneal blinks.")

add_q("inhouse_266", CAT9,
"A 56-year-old man with amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) undergoes cranial nerve examination. The physician places his index finger on the patient's chin with the mouth slightly open, and taps the finger briskly with a reflex hammer. The patient's mandible snaps upward with a forceful, hyperactive jerk (pathological jaw-jerk reflex). What are the afferent and efferent limbs of the jaw-jerk reflex, and what does a hyperactive reflex indicate?",
["Afferent: CN V3 (cell bodies in mesencephalic nucleus); Efferent: CN V3 motor fibers to masseter; indicates bilateral upper motor neuron lesion above the mid-pons (pseudobulbar palsy)", "Afferent: CN VII; Efferent: CN V3; indicates lower motor neuron lesion", "Afferent: CN IX; Efferent: CN X; indicates medullary infarction", "Afferent: CN V1; Efferent: CN VII; indicates cerebellar damage", "Afferent: CN XII; Efferent: CN XII; indicates tongue denervation"],
0,
"The JAW-JERK REFLEX (mandibular reflex) is a MONOSYNAPTIC STRETCH REFLEX mediated entirely by the MANDIBULAR DIVISION OF THE TRIGEMINAL NERVE (CN V3):\n1) AFFERENT LIMB: Tapping the chin suddenly stretches the masseter and temporalis muscles. Group Ia proprioceptive fibers travel via CN V3 with primary cell bodies in the MESENCEPHALIC NUCLEUS OF V;\n2) MONOSYNAPTIC CONNECTION: Mesencephalic axons project directly onto alpha motor neurons in the TRIGEMINAL MOTOR NUCLEUS in the mid-pons;\n3) EFFERENT LIMB: CN V3 motor axons travel to the masseter and temporalis, causing jaw closure.\nIn healthy individuals, the jaw-jerk reflex is MINIMAL OR ENTIRELY ABSENT. A BRISK, HYPERACTIVE JAW-JERK REFLEX indicates loss of descending corticobulbar inhibition due to a BILATERAL UPPER MOTOR NEURON LESION ROSTRAL TO THE MID-PONS (e.g., pseudobulbar palsy in ALS, MS, or bilateral hemispheric strokes).\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Afferent is CN V3, not CN VII; hyperactive reflex is UMN, not LMN.\n- CN IX and X mediate the gag reflex.\n- CN V1 and VII mediate the corneal reflex.\n- CN XII mediates tongue movements.")

add_q("inhouse_267", CAT9,
"A 61-year-old man presents with sudden vertigo, vomiting, severe difficulty swallowing (dysphagia), and hoarseness. Physical examination reveals loss of pain and temperature sensation on the LEFT side of his FACE and loss of pain and temperature sensation on the RIGHT side of his BODY ('crossed hemianalgesia'). He also has left-sided ptosis and miosis (Horner syndrome), left limb ataxia, and his uvula deviates to the right when he says 'ah.' Brain MRI confirms a lateral medullary infarction (Wallenberg syndrome). Occlusion of which artery is responsible, and which nucleus damage causes the dysphagia and hoarseness?",
["Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery (PICA); damage to the Nucleus Ambiguus", "Anterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery (AICA); damage to the Facial motor nucleus", "Superior Cerebellar Artery (SCA); damage to the Red nucleus", "Basilar artery; damage to the Abducens nucleus", "Anterior Spinal Artery (ASA); damage to the Hypoglossal nucleus"],
0,
"WALLENBERG SYNDROME (Lateral Medullary Syndrome) is caused by infarction of the lateral medulla, typically due to occlusion of the POSTERIOR INFERIOR CEREBELLAR ARTERY (PICA) or the vertebral artery. Key clinical features and damaged structures include:\n1) Spinal trigeminal nucleus and tract: IPSILATERAL loss of pain and temperature on the FACE;\n2) Spinothalamic tract (ALS): CONTRALATERAL loss of pain and temperature on the BODY ('crossed/alternating hemianalgesia');\n3) NUCLEUS AMBIGUUS (CN IX, X, XI motor): DYSPHAGIA, HOARSENESS, decreased gag reflex, and uvular deviation AWAY from the lesion;\n4) Vestibular nuclei: Vertigo, nystagmus, vomiting;\n5) Inferior cerebellar peduncle: Ipsilateral limb ataxia;\n6) Descending sympathetic fibers: Ipsilateral Horner syndrome (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis).\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- AICA occlusion causes Lateral Pontine Syndrome (prominent facial droop and hearing loss).\n- SCA supplies superior cerebellum and midbrain.\n- Basilar artery supplies ventral pons (locked-in syndrome).\n- ASA occlusion causes Medial Medullary Syndrome (contralateral hemiparesis, contralateral DCML loss, ipsilateral tongue deviation).")

add_q("inhouse_268", CAT9,
"A 58-year-old woman presents with acute vertigo, ataxia, and left facial weakness. Examination reveals an IPSILATERAL complete facial paralysis (inability to smile or close the left eye), ipsilateral sensorineural hearing loss and tinnitus, ipsilateral loss of facial pain and temperature sensation, ipsilateral Horner syndrome, and CONTRALATERAL loss of pain and temperature on the body. MRI confirms a Lateral Pontine Syndrome. Occlusion of which artery is responsible, and what key feature differentiates it from Wallenberg syndrome?",
["Anterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery (AICA); distinguished by IPSILATERAL FACIAL MOTOR PARALYSIS and SENSORINEURAL HEARING LOSS", "Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery (PICA); distinguished by dysphagia and hoarseness", "Posterior Cerebral Artery (PCA); distinguished by homonymous hemianopia", "Anterior Cerebral Artery (ACA); distinguished by urinary incontinence", "Middle Cerebral Artery (MCA); distinguished by aphasia"],
0,
"LATERAL PONTINE SYNDROME is caused by occlusion of the ANTERIOR INFERIOR CEREBELLAR ARTERY (AICA). Both AICA and PICA syndromes produce ataxia, Horner syndrome, ipsilateral facial sensory loss, and contralateral body pain/temperature loss. However, they are easily differentiated by their cranial nerve motor nuclei:\n- AICA (LATERAL PONS) damages the FACIAL MOTOR NUCLEUS (CN VII, producing IPSILATERAL FACIAL PARALYSIS / BELL'S PALSY-LIKE DROOP) and the COCHLEAR NUCLEI / LABYRINTHINE ARTERY (CN VIII, producing IPSILATERAL SENSORINEURAL HEARING LOSS and TINNITUS) (mnemonic: 'Facial droop = AICA Pooped');\n- PICA (LATERAL MEDULLA) damages the NUCLEUS AMBIGUUS (CN IX/X, producing DYSPHAGIA, HOARSENESS, and absent gag reflex; facial muscles and hearing are spared).\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- PICA causes Wallenberg syndrome with dysphagia/hoarseness, not facial paralysis.\n- PCA causes visual field cuts.\n- ACA causes lower extremity hemiplegia.\n- MCA causes face/arm hemiplegia and aphasia.")

add_q("inhouse_269", CAT9,
"A pain medicine specialist explains the pathophysiological distinction between nociceptive pain and neuropathic pain. A patient presents with burning, shooting, electric-shock-like pain across his foot following surgical trauma to a peripheral cutaneous nerve. How does neuropathic pain differ fundamentally in its origin from nociceptive pain?",
["Neuropathic pain is caused by a primary lesion, injury, or disease affecting the somatosensory nervous system itself, characterized by spontaneous firing and altered excitability", "Neuropathic pain is caused by physiological stimulation of undamaged nociceptors by inflammatory prostaglandins", "Neuropathic pain is an imaginary psychiatric symptom lacking anatomical basis", "Neuropathic pain is mediated exclusively by visceral sympathetic afferents", "Neuropathic pain is completely abolished by over-the-counter NSAIDs"],
0,
"The IASP defines NEUROPATHIC PAIN as pain caused by a primary lesion, dysfunction, or disease affecting the SOMATOSENSORY NERVOUS SYSTEM itself (either peripheral, such as diabetic polyneuropathy or radiculopathy; or central, such as post-stroke thalamic pain or MS plaques). Unlike NOCICEPTIVE PAIN (which is a normal physiological warning signal elicited by noxious stimulation of intact receptors in inflamed tissues, like arthritis or burns), neuropathic pain is characterized by maladaptive neuroplastic changes: spontaneous ectopic pacemaker firing of injured axons, upregulation of voltage-gated Na+ channels, loss of GABAergic inhibitory interneurons, and central sensitization. Clinically, it is described as burning, lancinating, shooting, or electric shocks, with allodynia and hyperalgesia. It is typically poorly responsive to NSAIDs and opioids, requiring neuromodulators like gabapentinoids, SNRIs, or TCAs.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Inflammatory stimulation of intact receptors describes NOCICEPTIVE/inflammatory pain.\n- Neuropathic pain is a well-established organic neurological condition, not an imaginary psychiatric symptom.\n- It affects somatic as well as autonomic pathways.\n- It is notoriously resistant to NSAIDs.")

add_q("inhouse_270", CAT9,
"Following a localized scald burn on his forearm, a patient notes that the red, inflamed skin surrounding the burn has a significantly lowered pain threshold, such that even mild warmth or light pressure provokes sharp pain. This peripheral sensitization is driven by chemical mediators released in the 'inflammatory soup.' Which combination of substances contributes directly to peripheral sensitization of primary nociceptive nerve terminals?",
["Substance P, CGRP, Bradykinin, Prostaglandin E2, Histamine, and Protons (H+)", "Acetylcholine, Dopamine, and Epinephrine exclusively", "GABA, Glycine, and Endorphins", "Thyroid hormone, Insulin, and Glucagon", "Myelin basic protein and Oligodendrocyte transcription factors"],
0,
"PERIPHERAL SENSITIZATION is the reduction in threshold and increase in responsiveness of primary afferent nociceptors (A-delta and C fibers) at the site of tissue injury and inflammation. It is mediated by the 'INFLAMMATORY SOUP' released by damaged cells, platelets, mast cells, macrophages, and sensory terminals via axon reflex:\n- PROSTAGLANDINS (PGE2, PGI2 from COX-2): Phosphorylate Nav1.8 and Nav1.9 channels via PKA/PKC, lowering activation threshold;\n- BRADYKININ: Directly activates B1 and B2 GPCRs on nociceptors;\n- SUBSTANCE P and CGRP: Released antidromically, causing vasodilation, plasma extravasation (neurogenic inflammation), and mast cell degranulation;\n- HISTAMINE (from mast cells) and SEROTONIN (from platelets);\n- PROTONS (H+, tissue acidosis) and ATP: Directly open TRPV1 and P2X3 ion channels, sensitizing nerve terminals.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Central monoamines are not the defining components of the peripheral inflammatory soup.\n- GABA, glycine, and endorphins are INHIBITORY analgesics, not sensitizers.\n- Thyroid hormone, insulin, and glucagon are endocrine metabolic hormones.\n- Myelin proteins do not act as local inflammatory sensitizers.")

add_q("inhouse_271", CAT9,
"In chronic neuropathic pain states, persistent, repetitive firing of unmyelinated C-fiber nociceptors produces a progressive, long-lasting increase in the excitability of second-order dorsal horn neurons, known as 'central sensitization' or the 'wind-up' phenomenon. What synaptic neurotransmitter and receptor system in the dorsal horn is the critical molecular driver of this central sensitization?",
["Repetitive glutamate release that causes sustained depolarization, relieving the voltage-dependent Mg2+ block from postsynaptic NMDA receptors and allowing massive Ca2+ influx", "Continuous glycine release that hyperpolarizes dorsal horn interneurons", "Upregulation of GABA-A receptors leading to increased chloride conductance", "Inhibition of all voltage-gated sodium channels in the substantia gelatinosa", "Degradation of AMPA receptors by matrix metalloproteinases"],
0,
"CENTRAL SENSITIZATION is an activity-dependent increase in the excitability and synaptic efficacy of neurons in the spinal cord dorsal horn (and spinal trigeminal nucleus pars caudalis). During intense, persistent C-fiber nociceptive input:\n1) Massive release of GLUTAMATE and neurokinins (substance P) causes prolonged postsynaptic depolarization via AMPA and neurokinin-1 (NK-1) receptors;\n2) This sustained depolarization expels the VOLTAGE-DEPENDENT MAGNESIUM (Mg2+) PLUG from POSTSYNAPTIC NMDA RECEPTORS;\n3) Unblocked NMDA receptors conduct a massive INFLUX OF CALCIUM (Ca2+) into second-order neurons ('WIND-UP' phenomenon);\n4) Intracellular calcium activates CaMKII, PKC, and MAP kinases, which phosphorylate AMPA receptors (increasing single-channel conductance and membrane insertion) and inactivate inhibitory GABA/glycine circuits. As a result, dorsal horn pain-transmission neurons become hyperexcitable, firing in response to previously subthreshold tactile inputs (allodynia) and expanding their receptive fields.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Glycine release produces inhibition and analgesia, the opposite of sensitization.\n- GABA-A upregulation produces inhibition, whereas central sensitization involves loss of GABAergic inhibition (disinhibition).\n- Sodium channels are upregulated, not inhibited.\n- AMPA receptors are inserted and upregulated, not degraded.")

add_q("inhouse_272", CAT9,
"A 60-year-old woman with postherpetic neuralgia reports that even when the soft cotton fabric of her nightgown lightly brushes across her thoracic cage, she experiences excruciating, burning pain that causes her to scream. A light touch that is normally non-painful provoking severe pain is the definition of which clinical sensory abnormality, and which nerve fibers mediate this input?",
["Allodynia; mediated centrally by low-threshold A-beta tactile fibers activating sensitized dorsal horn pain circuits", "Hyperalgesia; mediated by high-threshold C-fibers responding to heat", "Paresthesia; mediated by unmyelinated sympathetic fibers", "Hypalgesia; mediated by motor efferent fibers", "Anesthesia dolorosa; mediated by destroyed sensory nerves"],
0,
"ALLODYNIA is defined by the IASP as 'pain due to a stimulus that does not normally provoke pain' (e.g., pain provoked by the gentle brushing of a cotton wisp, clothing, or a light breeze against the skin). In the normal, uninjured nervous system, light touch is conducted by low-threshold myelinated A-BETA FIBERS that synapse on dorsal horn interneurons that signal touch, not pain. However, in the setting of CENTRAL SENSITIZATION, synaptic reorganization occurs in the dorsal horn: inhibitory GABAergic/glycinergic gating is lost, and low-threshold A-beta fibers establish functional synaptic connections with sensitized nociceptive second-order spinothalamic neurons. Consequently, harmless tactile inputs from A-beta fibers directly activate the ascending pain pathway, triggering conscious perception of severe pain.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Hyperalgesia is an exaggerated, increased pain response to a stimulus that is ALREADY NOXIOUS (e.g., severe pain from a minor pinprick).\n- Paresthesias are spontaneous abnormal non-painful sensations (tingling, pins-and-needles).\n- Hypalgesia is diminished sensitivity to pain.\n- Anesthesia dolorosa is spontaneous pain in an area that is completely numb to all testing.")

add_q("inhouse_273", CAT9,
"A patient with a mild superficial abrasion on his arm is tested with a sharp neurological safety pin. When the pin lightly pricks the uninjured skin 10 cm away, he rates the pain as 2/10. However, when the exact same pinprick is applied 1 cm outside the edge of the abrasion, he wincing and rates the pain as 8/10. What clinical sensory abnormality does this illustrate, and how is it classified?",
["Secondary hyperalgesia (increased pain response in undamaged tissue surrounding an injury, mediated by central sensitization)", "Primary allodynia (pain evoked by non-painful touch)", "Dysesthesia (unpleasant non-painful tingling)", "Hyperpathia (delayed explosive pain)", "Phantom pain (pain in an absent limb)"],
0,
"HYPERALGESIA is defined as 'an increased pain response to a stimulus that is normally painful' (e.g., a pinprick that normally hurts mildly causing severe pain). It is classified into two distinct forms:\n1) PRIMARY HYPERALGESIA: Occurs directly WITHIN THE BOUNDARIES OF THE TISSUE INJURY (the burn or wound). It is driven primarily by PERIPHERAL SENSITIZATION (sensitization of primary nociceptors by the local inflammatory soup);\n2) SECONDARY HYPERALGESIA: Occurs in the NORMAL, UNDAMAGED TISSUE SURROUNDING THE INJURY ZONE. Because the skin surrounding the wound is physically uninjured, secondary hyperalgesia cannot be caused by peripheral inflammation; it is driven entirely by CENTRAL SENSITIZATION in the spinal cord dorsal horn, where the receptive fields of second-order spinothalamic neurons have expanded.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- Allodynia is pain evoked by a NON-painful stimulus (like light touch); a pinprick is an inherently noxious stimulus.\n- Dysesthesia is an unpleasant abnormal sensation, not specific for heightened pinprick response.\n- Hyperpathia is characterized by high threshold and explosive after-discharge.\n- Phantom pain requires limb loss.")

add_q("inhouse_274", CAT9,
"A 56-year-old woman presents with paroxysms of agonizing, lightning-like, electric-shock pain across the right side of her face involving her cheek, upper lip, and upper teeth (V2 distribution). Each attack lasts 5 to 15 seconds, occurs dozens of times daily, and is triggered by light touch, brushing her teeth, chewing food, or talking. Between attacks, she has zero pain and her neurological examination is completely normal. Brain MRI with constructive interference in steady state (CISS) sequence demonstrates compression of the right trigeminal nerve root by which anatomical structure, and what is the first-line medical therapy?",
["Vascular compression by an aberrant loop of the Superior Cerebellar Artery (SCA); first-line therapy is CARBAMAZEPINE", "Compression by the Posterior Cerebral Artery; first-line therapy is High-dose Aspirin", "Acoustic neuroma; first-line therapy is Radiation therapy", "Dural arteriovenous fistula; first-line therapy is Warfarin", "Compression by the Internal Carotid Artery; first-line therapy is Sumatriptan"],
0,
"TRIGEMINAL NEURALGIA (Tic Douloureux) is characterized by excruciating, paroxysmal, unilateral, electric-shock-like lancinating pain restricted to one or more divisions of the trigeminal nerve (most commonly V2 and/or V3, rarely V1). Attacks last seconds to 2 minutes and are triggered by innocuous tactile stimulation of 'trigger zones' (shaving, brushing teeth, chewing, wind). Neurological examination is completely normal. In >80-90% of classic cases, high-resolution MRI reveals NEUROVASCULAR COMPRESSION of the trigeminal nerve root entry zone in the prepontine cistern, overwhelmingly caused by an ectatic or aberrant loop of the SUPERIOR CEREBELLAR ARTERY (SCA). Vascular pulsation causes focal demyelination, allowing ephaptic cross-talk between A-beta tactile fibers and nociceptive fibers. The established first-line medical therapy is CARBAMAZEPINE (blocks voltage-gated Na+ channels; side effects: aplastic anemia, agranulocytosis, SIADH, Stevens-Johnson syndrome in HLA-B*1502). Surgical microvascular decompression (Jannetta procedure) is curative.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- PCA courses above the oculomotor nerve in the midbrain, not the trigeminal nerve in the prepontine cistern; aspirin is ineffective.\n- Acoustic neuroma causes hearing loss and tinnitus (CN VIII) and persistent numbness, not classic paroxysmal tic douloureux.\n- Warfarin is an anticoagulant and does not treat neuralgic pain.\n- Sumatriptan is for acute migraine, not trigeminal neuralgia.")

add_q("inhouse_275", CAT9,
"Six months after a non-displaced Colles fracture of her right distal radius, a 52-year-old woman presents to the pain clinic with severe, continuous burning and throbbing pain in her right hand that is grossly out of proportion to the original injury. Physical examination reveals exquisite allodynia (screaming when a cotton swab touches her skin), diffuse non-pitting edema of the hand, mottled red-purple skin that is cold to the touch, hyperhidrosis (excessive sweating), and brittle, ridged fingernails with patchy osteopenia on hand radiographs. Electrodiagnostic testing reveals normal nerve conduction velocities with NO evidence of a specific major peripheral nerve injury. What is the diagnosis?",
["Complex Regional Pain Syndrome Type 1 (CRPS-1 / Reflex Sympathetic Dystrophy)", "Complex Regional Pain Syndrome Type 2 (Causalgia)", "Carpal tunnel syndrome", "Diabetic polyneuropathy", "Rheumatoid arthritis"],
0,
"COMPLEX REGIONAL PAIN SYNDROME (CRPS) is a debilitating neuropathic pain condition that typically develops in an extremity following trauma, fracture, sprain, or surgery. The diagnostic hallmark is pain that is DISPROPORTIONATE IN SEVERITY AND DURATION to the inciting event. It is categorized into two distinct subtypes:\n1) CRPS TYPE 1 (historically REFLEX SYMPATHETIC DYSTROPHY / RSD): Develops in the ABSENCE OF A DOCUMENTED MAJOR PERIPHERAL NERVE LESION (e.g., following a Colles fracture or ankle sprain);\n2) CRPS TYPE 2 (historically CAUSALGIA): Develops following a DOCUMENTED, SPECIFIC MAJOR PERIPHERAL NERVE TRUNK INJURY (e.g., median or sciatic nerve transection).\nBoth types share the classic tetrad of symptoms: sensory (burning pain, severe allodynia, hyperalgesia), autonomic/vasomotor (skin color changes, temperature asymmetry >1°C, edema), sudomotor (hyperhidrosis or hypohidrosis), and trophic changes (atrophic shiny skin, altered hair/nail growth, osteopenia). Pathophysiology involves neurogenic inflammation, autonomic dysregulation, and central sensitization.\n\nDistractor rationales:\n- CRPS Type 2 requires a documented nerve injury (e.g., median nerve laceration).\n- Carpal tunnel causes numbness in median distribution without diffuse extremity edema, autonomic trophic changes, and regional osteopenia.\n- Diabetic polyneuropathy is bilateral symmetric and distal, without acute post-fracture presentation.\n- Rheumatoid arthritis causes inflammatory synovitis without prominent burning allodynia and sudomotor autonomic storm.")

